WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.010 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.870 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Early
on in this class,

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:15.320 align:middle line:84%
I think, I gave you examples--
it might have exactly

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.309 align:middle line:90%
been these examples.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:18.350 align:middle line:84%
I meant to look back at
the old slides to see.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.780 align:middle line:84%
But it was something with
this character anyway.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.370 align:middle line:90%
I invited you to tell me--

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:27.110 align:middle line:84%
if I ask you a question like
"Who did Biden ask to see him?"

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.180 align:middle line:90%
I asked you, who's "him"?

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.650 align:middle line:90%
And I think, at the time--

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.410 align:middle line:84%
who knows, we could do this
exercise again and find out

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.760 align:middle line:84%
whether your minds have been
warped by a semester of 24.900.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.410 align:middle line:84%
I think, at the
time, what you said

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.600 align:middle line:84%
was that "him" could
refer to Biden.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.457 align:middle line:84%
It was possible for
this to mean "who"'s

00:00:43.457 --> 00:00:45.290 align:middle line:84%
the person such that
Biden asked that person

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.710 align:middle line:84%
to come to his, that is Biden's,
office and see him, right?

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.510 align:middle line:90%
It could mean that.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.190 align:middle line:84%
And then so it could also refer
to someone else, of course.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:56.540 align:middle line:90%
But it could refer to Biden.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.900 align:middle line:84%
We weren't doing
this at the time,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.150 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of the class.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.990 align:middle line:84%
But we can represent that
with these subscripts,

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.750 align:middle line:84%
since we've since talked
about binding theory.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.475 align:middle line:84%
This is the kind of fact we're
in a position to talk about.

00:01:09.475 --> 00:01:11.600 align:middle line:84%
And then what we also did
at the beginning of class

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.780 align:middle line:84%
was to think also about
questions like "Why did Biden

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.370 align:middle line:90%
ask to see him?"

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.350 align:middle line:84%
And what you all
agreed at the time--

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.798 align:middle line:84%
and feel free to jump in
and stop me if you-- oh,

00:01:21.798 --> 00:01:22.840 align:middle line:90%
Faith's about to stop me.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:23.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was just
going to be like, I

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.208 align:middle line:84%
feel like now with
everything we've

00:01:25.208 --> 00:01:27.440 align:middle line:84%
learned about wh-movement,
it feels wrong

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.190 align:middle line:84%
because you're moving something
out of an island, I think.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.683 align:middle line:84%
But you're keeping it
there at the same time.

00:01:32.683 --> 00:01:34.400 align:middle line:84%
Like "him," I feel
like it was more

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.750 align:middle line:84%
appropriate to omit
"him" because you're

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:38.962 align:middle line:90%
moving the "who" anyway.

00:01:38.962 --> 00:01:40.420 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Oh, the first one?

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.920 align:middle line:90%
"Who did Biden ask to see him?"

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.580 align:middle line:84%
Oh, so in the first one, "Who
did Biden ask to see him?"

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:51.970 align:middle line:84%
is supposed-- the answer
is supposed to be the--

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:55.600 align:middle line:84%
whatever, the head of the
Joint Chiefs of Staff.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.910 align:middle line:84%
He asked the head of the
Joint Chiefs of Staff

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.620 align:middle line:90%
to come and see him, Biden.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.810 align:middle line:84%
So Biden's worried
about something

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.790 align:middle line:84%
that he wants that
guy's advice on.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.280 align:middle line:84%
And so he asked him
to come see him.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.820 align:middle line:84%
I think that's an OK question
that you can ask that way.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:12.370 align:middle line:84%
Yes, so you have been
warped, you specifically,

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:14.350 align:middle line:90%
by a semester of 24.900.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.380 align:middle line:84%
You're starting to fear islands
everywhere, which is good.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.240 align:middle line:84%
It's an important
part of your training

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.690 align:middle line:90%
as a syntactician, yeah.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:23.200 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.812 align:middle line:84%
So the first one then,
"him" can refer to Biden,

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:26.770 align:middle line:90%
but it doesn't have to.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.395 align:middle line:84%
For the second one,
I think what people

00:02:28.395 --> 00:02:31.300 align:middle line:84%
said at the time was that
it wasn't possible for "him"

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.190 align:middle line:90%
to refer to Biden anymore.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:36.010 align:middle line:84%
So "Why did Biden
ask to see him?"

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.540 align:middle line:84%
That has to be,
"Why did Biden ask

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.120 align:middle line:84%
to see some person,
some particular person,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.440 align:middle line:90%
but not Biden?"

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.690 align:middle line:84%
Do people still
have that feeling?

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:47.570 align:middle line:84%
It's been a whole semester of
suffering through this stuff.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:48.560 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.930 align:middle line:90%
Yes, many of you do.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.680 align:middle line:84%
Some of you, it's too
early in the morning

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.030 align:middle line:84%
to have feelings
about binding theory.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.790 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to
have feelings exactly.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.200 align:middle line:90%
I'll settle for opinions.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.280 align:middle line:84%
So I think there's this contrast
between these sentences.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.380 align:middle line:84%
And you know what I said
at the beginning of class

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.750 align:middle line:84%
was look, these sentences,
they look pretty darn

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.410 align:middle line:84%
similar-- they only vary
in, like, one vowel, right?

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:12.020 align:middle line:84%
It's the difference
between "who" and "why."

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.395 align:middle line:84%
And yet, you make
that small change

00:03:14.395 --> 00:03:15.770 align:middle line:84%
to the first word
in the sentence

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.230 align:middle line:84%
and the last word in
the sentence changes

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.060 align:middle line:90%
who it can refer to.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.900 align:middle line:84%
Pretty complicated
array of facts.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.307 align:middle line:84%
And I think what I also
promised, incautiously,

00:03:24.307 --> 00:03:26.390 align:middle line:84%
was that by the end of
class, you would understand

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.200 align:middle line:90%
why it worked this way.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.840 align:middle line:84%
And so I just wanted
to stop and talk

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.060 align:middle line:84%
about why it worked
this way, partly

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.560 align:middle line:84%
as a way of segueing
into what we're

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.010 align:middle line:84%
going to talk about today,
which is language acquisition.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:40.600 align:middle line:90%
So why does it work this way?

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.505 align:middle line:84%
So first, as Faith
just said, one

00:03:42.505 --> 00:03:44.380 align:middle line:84%
of the things we know
about the first example

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.860 align:middle line:84%
is that "who" is moving, it's
starting off as the object of

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:48.550 align:middle line:90%
"ask."

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:52.480 align:middle line:84%
So "Biden asked the
chairman to see him."

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.120 align:middle line:84%
"The chairman" is
the object of "ask."

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.953 align:middle line:84%
Instead of "the chairman,"
you've got "who."

00:03:55.953 --> 00:03:57.640 align:middle line:84%
And "who" is
undergoing wh-movement.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:00.670 align:middle line:84%
"Why" is presumably also
undergoing wh-movement.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.770 align:middle line:84%
Let's not worry about
where it's coming from.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.490 align:middle line:90%


00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.910 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing
that maybe you're

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:11.030 align:middle line:84%
in a position to
believe by now--

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:13.940 align:middle line:84%
we talked about this when we
were doing binding theory--

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.250 align:middle line:84%
was that these infinitival
clauses, although they

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.709 align:middle line:84%
don't appear to have subjects,
that they actually do

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:19.459 align:middle line:90%
have subjects.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.470 align:middle line:90%
Their subjects are just null.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:22.670 align:middle line:90%
We were calling it "PRO."

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.270 align:middle line:84%
It's this null pronoun that
refers back, in both cases,

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.230 align:middle line:84%
to something in
the higher clause.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:33.080 align:middle line:84%
So in the first example, "Who
did Biden ask to see him?"

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.900 align:middle line:84%
We consider the non-wh-moved
version of this--

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:46.100 align:middle line:84%
"Biden asked Harris
PRO to see him."

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.100 align:middle line:90%


00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.160 align:middle line:84%
Here, PRO refers
to Harris, right?

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.550 align:middle line:84%
This means Biden asked Harris
to do the following thing.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.693 align:middle line:84%
He wanted Harris
to come see him.

00:04:57.693 --> 00:04:58.610 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound right?

00:04:58.610 --> 00:04:59.580 align:middle line:90%
That's what that means.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.630 align:middle line:84%
So that PRO is an instance of
what we called object control.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:05.420 align:middle line:84%
PRO is referring back to an
object in the higher clause.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:06.666 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:05:06.666 --> 00:05:07.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.582 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's OK.

00:05:08.582 --> 00:05:11.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: PRO in
to satisfy the EPP.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.620 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, that's right.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:13.610 align:middle line:90%
That's what it was for.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:15.500 align:middle line:90%
It satisfies the EPP.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.290 align:middle line:84%
C needs a subject and
doesn't appear to have one.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.200 align:middle line:84%
And the claim back then was
it really does have one.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.433 align:middle line:84%
We went through this
idea very quickly.

00:05:23.433 --> 00:05:25.100 align:middle line:84%
One of the things we
discovered about it

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:29.000 align:middle line:84%
was that it made the statement
of binding theory simpler.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.670 align:middle line:84%
It made it easier
to understand why

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:33.920 align:middle line:84%
pronouns and anaphores
inside infinitives

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.480 align:middle line:90%
act the way they do.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.890 align:middle line:84%
And this is, in a way,
an instance of that.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.130 align:middle line:90%
So this PRO is referring back--

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.710 align:middle line:84%
I'll give it the same subscript
that it's got up there.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:46.355 align:middle line:84%
If we change "Harris"
to "who" and move it--

00:05:46.355 --> 00:05:48.890 align:middle line:90%


00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:52.100 align:middle line:90%
"Who did Biden ask to see him?"

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:56.180 align:middle line:84%
This PRO is controlled--
we say it refers back

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.330 align:middle line:84%
to the object of
the higher clause.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.550 align:middle line:84%
In the second example,
the higher verb, "ask,"

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:02.575 align:middle line:90%
doesn't have an object.

00:06:02.575 --> 00:06:04.680 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have
to have an object.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.040 align:middle line:84%
So you can say "Biden
asks Harris to see him."

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:15.230 align:middle line:84%
Can also say "Biden asked--"
this is with no object--

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:15.995 align:middle line:90%
"to see him."

00:06:15.995 --> 00:06:18.870 align:middle line:90%


00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:23.280 align:middle line:84%
And then the PRO gets
controlled by the subject.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.278 align:middle line:84%
So "ask," when it
has an object, it's

00:06:25.278 --> 00:06:26.820 align:middle line:84%
what's called an
object control verb.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.828 align:middle line:84%
The object of
"ask" controls PRO.

00:06:28.828 --> 00:06:30.870 align:middle line:84%
When it doesn't have an
object, it's the subject.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:34.035 align:middle line:84%
I think I said when we very
briefly talked about this,

00:06:34.035 --> 00:06:38.670 align:middle line:84%
it sure would be nice to
understand why a verb--

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:42.390 align:middle line:84%
what it is that conditions,
which thing controls PRO.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.970 align:middle line:84%
That's a topic of
serious research.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.430 align:middle line:84%
People try to figure out
what the rules for that are.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.575 align:middle line:90%
And it's not simple.

00:06:50.575 --> 00:06:52.450 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, this is a way
of representing what's

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:53.770 align:middle line:90%
going on in these sentences.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:57.160 align:middle line:84%
Those embedded clauses
have a subject,

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:59.200 align:middle line:84%
a subject you can't
see, that refers back

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.600 align:middle line:84%
either to the object of
"ask," in the first example,

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:06.720 align:middle line:84%
or to the subject of "ask,"
Biden, in the second example.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.860 align:middle line:90%
This has been review, sort of.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:10.360 align:middle line:90%
Does this make sense?

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.540 align:middle line:90%
Do people sort of remember this?

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.570 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:16.400 align:middle line:84%
And this, finally,
allows us to understand

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.920 align:middle line:84%
why the pronoun at the end
is behaving the way it is.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:22.370 align:middle line:84%
So there's this general
principle, principle B,

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:25.490 align:middle line:84%
that says pronouns have to
be free in a certain domain.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:27.440 align:middle line:84%
That is, they have
to not have a binder,

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.690 align:middle line:84%
not have something that
c-commands them and corefers

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:30.320 align:middle line:90%
with them.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:31.900 align:middle line:90%
That's too close.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:34.790 align:middle line:84%
We have to figure out
what "too close" means.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:36.260 align:middle line:84%
We can account for
these data if we

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:39.260 align:middle line:84%
decide that the domain in which
the pronoun has to be free

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.360 align:middle line:84%
is the domain that I've
got brackets around,

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.160 align:middle line:90%
the embedded clause.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:46.470 align:middle line:84%
So it has to be
free in that clause.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:47.180 align:middle line:90%
I don't care.

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.230 align:middle line:90%
Go away.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:50.240 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.370 align:middle line:84%
It has to be free
in that clause.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:54.280 align:middle line:90%
What does that mean?

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:58.050 align:middle line:84%
Well, in the first
example, it just

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.998 align:middle line:84%
means that "him" has
to not refer to Harris.

00:08:00.998 --> 00:08:02.790 align:middle line:84%
Well, there are lots
of good reasons for it

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:03.748 align:middle line:90%
not to refer to Harris.

00:08:03.748 --> 00:08:06.195 align:middle line:90%
I should have used a male name.

00:08:06.195 --> 00:08:09.150 align:middle line:84%
It can't refer to the
object of the higher clause

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.030 align:middle line:84%
because that object is
controlling this pro.

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:17.100 align:middle line:84%
So if we kept Harris,
if we said "Biden

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.110 align:middle line:84%
asked Harris to see him,"
then "him," of course,

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:19.830 align:middle line:90%
couldn't refer to Harris.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.140 align:middle line:84%
But if you said "Biden
asked Harris to see her,"

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.570 align:middle line:84%
then "her" still couldn't
refer to Harris--

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.450 align:middle line:84%
background knowledge for anybody
who has forgotten this Harris

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:29.850 align:middle line:90%
is female.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.350 align:middle line:84%
"Her" still couldn't
refer to Harris

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:34.990 align:middle line:84%
because "her" is too close to
PRO would be the story, yeah.

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:37.080 align:middle line:84%
So that "him" is capable
of referring to Biden

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.747 align:middle line:84%
because "Biden" is outside
the bracketed domain.

00:08:40.747 --> 00:08:42.539 align:middle line:84%
What about the "him"
in the second example?

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:44.414 align:middle line:84%
Well, "Biden" is outside
the bracketed domain

00:08:44.414 --> 00:08:45.370 align:middle line:90%
in that example, too.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:49.350 align:middle line:84%
But there is a PRO in
the bracketed domain,

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.870 align:middle line:84%
the embedded clause, that
is controlled by "Biden"

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:52.920 align:middle line:90%
and refers to Biden.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:55.352 align:middle line:84%
So "him" cannot refer to
Biden because there's-- not

00:08:55.352 --> 00:08:57.810 align:middle line:84%
because "Biden" is too close,
but because PRO is too close.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.510 align:middle line:84%
PRO is inside the
bracketed domain.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.990 align:middle line:90%
And it binds him.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.580 align:middle line:90%
It's too close.

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:05.180 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:06.500 align:middle line:90%
People remember principle B?

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.375 align:middle line:84%
Principle B was meant
to account for the fact

00:09:08.375 --> 00:09:22.400 align:middle line:84%
that if I say "Biden
thinks Harris likes him,"

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.520 align:middle line:90%
that "him" can refer to Biden.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:27.950 align:middle line:90%
"Biden" is far enough away.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:38.840 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if I say "Harris thinks
Biden likes him," now "him"

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:39.860 align:middle line:90%
cannot refer to Biden.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:41.210 align:middle line:90%
"Biden" is too close.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:43.910 align:middle line:84%
So we have a principle
B that says "him"--

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:47.120 align:middle line:84%
pronouns generally-- can't
refer to a c-commanding noun

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:48.530 align:middle line:90%
phrase that's too close.

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:49.910 align:middle line:90%
We have to define "too close."

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:53.370 align:middle line:84%
But it's something like
inside the same clause.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.640 align:middle line:84%
So here, "Biden" is far
enough away for "him"

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:56.420 align:middle line:90%
to refer to Biden.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:57.212 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't have to.

00:09:57.212 --> 00:09:58.730 align:middle line:90%
It could be somebody else.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.640 align:middle line:84%
Here, "Biden" is too close
and "him" can't refer to it.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:04.815 align:middle line:84%
And something similar is
going on in the slide.

00:10:04.815 --> 00:10:06.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is me making
good on my promise

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:07.490 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning
of the semester.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:09.600 align:middle line:84%
Beginning the semester, I said,
by the end of the semester,

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:10.940 align:middle line:90%
you'll understand why this is.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:12.875 align:middle line:90%
So now, you do, right?

00:10:12.875 --> 00:10:14.320 align:middle line:84%
Do you have
questions about this?

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:15.195 align:middle line:90%
Does this make sense?

00:10:15.195 --> 00:10:17.660 align:middle line:90%


00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:21.950 align:middle line:84%
Well, so now we understand
this-- yeah, yeah,

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:23.690 align:middle line:90%
I did all that.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:24.860 align:middle line:90%
Now, we understand all this.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:30.600 align:middle line:84%
But you know, all of you are
exceptionally smart, right?

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.450 align:middle line:84%
And here you are getting an
expensive university education,

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.720 align:middle line:84%
where someone is explaining
this to you in great detail.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.760 align:middle line:84%
And so that's why you
understand what's going on here.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.492 align:middle line:84%
But there's another sense in
which you understood something

00:10:43.492 --> 00:10:44.700 align:middle line:90%
about what was going on here.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:45.930 align:middle line:90%
You had these intuitions.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.740 align:middle line:84%
You knew what those pronouns
could refer to before you ever

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:51.210 align:middle line:90%
set foot in this class.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:54.240 align:middle line:84%
And people who don't have your
advantages, people who are not

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.340 align:middle line:84%
getting expensive university
educations, people

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:59.760 align:middle line:84%
who are not as smart as you,
they also have those judgments.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.103 align:middle line:84%
You can wander around Boston,
ask any native speaker

00:11:03.103 --> 00:11:05.520 align:middle line:84%
of English, they'll agree about
these sentences, about who

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:08.080 align:middle line:90%
"him" refers to, yeah?

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.140 align:middle line:84%
So question-- how
did that happen?

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:17.070 align:middle line:84%
How do you end up with this,
in the sense, exceptionally

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:18.960 align:middle line:90%
complicated knowledge?

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.540 align:middle line:90%
How are you doing all of this?

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.550 align:middle line:84%
This is a subpart
of what Chomsky

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.190 align:middle line:90%
likes to call Plato's problem.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:30.000 align:middle line:84%
Plato, I guess, in his writings
somewhere asked this question--

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.890 align:middle line:84%
how is it that--
he wasn't talking

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:33.210 align:middle line:90%
about language specifically.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.830 align:middle line:84%
How is it that we know
so much about the world,

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:39.180 align:middle line:84%
despite the fact
that our evidence is

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.100 align:middle line:90%
kind of random and spotty?

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:44.040 align:middle line:84%
It's not as if your parents
ever sat you down and explained,

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:48.360 align:middle line:84%
"Oh by the way, if I say
'Biden asked to see him,' then

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.843 align:middle line:90%
'him' can't refer to Biden."

00:11:50.843 --> 00:11:52.260 align:middle line:84%
Nobody ever explained
that to you.

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:55.320 align:middle line:84%
You just kind of figured
it out, despite the lack

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.110 align:middle line:90%
of any relevant instruction--

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:01.260 align:middle line:84%
not just this fact, but a whole
bunch of complicated facts, all

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:04.770 align:middle line:84%
the complicated stuff we've been
talking about in this class.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:07.830 align:middle line:84%
Your parents never explained
that to you, I assume.

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:08.410 align:middle line:90%
Who knows?

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:09.910 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's
somebody in this class

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:12.360 align:middle line:84%
whose parents were exceptionally
helpful about this stuff.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.520 align:middle line:84%
But presumably, not all of you
had your parents explain this

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:16.020 align:middle line:90%
to you.

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:19.200 align:middle line:90%


00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.720 align:middle line:84%
People who have studied
the interactions of parents

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:26.910 align:middle line:84%
with children, it's pretty clear
that parents very occasionally

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:33.000 align:middle line:84%
give children direct
instruction about how to speak.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.170 align:middle line:84%
But they usually have
other kinds of priorities.

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:38.490 align:middle line:84%
So this is an actual dialogue
between a child and the mother.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:41.100 align:middle line:84%
The child says, "Mama
isn't a boy, he a girl."

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.945 align:middle line:90%
The mother says, "That's right."

00:12:42.945 --> 00:12:45.300 align:middle line:84%
The child says, and "Walt
Disney comes on Tuesday."

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:46.830 align:middle line:84%
The mother says,
"No, he does not."

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:49.558 align:middle line:84%
So this is an actual dialogue
between a small child

00:12:49.558 --> 00:12:50.100 align:middle line:90%
and a mother.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.683 align:middle line:84%
And something that
you notice about it

00:12:51.683 --> 00:12:54.300 align:middle line:84%
is that if the child is
relying on the parent

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:58.560 align:middle line:84%
to get explicit
instruction about grammar,

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.530 align:middle line:84%
then the child is
out of luck, right?

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:06.090 align:middle line:84%
So the child's first sentence
has some grammatical problems.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.250 align:middle line:84%
The child is using
"he" to refer to--

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.410 align:middle line:90%
to refer to the mother.

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:12.235 align:middle line:84%
The response that
the mother gets--

00:13:12.235 --> 00:13:13.860 align:middle line:84%
the child gets from
the mother for that

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:16.050 align:middle line:90%
is "That's right," right?

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:19.170 align:middle line:84%
Because the mother is
not thinking too much

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:21.570 align:middle line:90%
about the grammar.

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:24.030 align:middle line:84%
The child has made an accurate
observation about the world,

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.250 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:27.177 align:middle line:84%
We can worry later
about pronouns.

00:13:27.177 --> 00:13:29.010 align:middle line:84%
The point is the child
is figuring something

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.320 align:middle line:84%
out about the gender
of their mother.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.840 align:middle line:84%
And then the child says this
perfectly grammatical sentence

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:34.260 align:middle line:90%
at the end--

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:35.718 align:middle line:84%
"And Walt Disney
comes on Tuesday."

00:13:35.718 --> 00:13:38.010 align:middle line:84%
And the mother says, "No,
you're wrong," right?

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:41.658 align:middle line:84%
Well, OK, so
apparently, the mother

00:13:41.658 --> 00:13:43.950 align:middle line:84%
is not paying too much
attention to stuff like grammar.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:47.250 align:middle line:84%
The mother is paying attention
to accuracy about the world.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.560 align:middle line:84%
And this child is
therefore going

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.200 align:middle line:84%
to grow up to be a normal
and well-adjusted human being

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:55.200 align:middle line:84%
because that's what you
want from your parents.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.190 align:middle line:84%
It would be odd for the mother
to say "No,, no, you're using

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:00.810 align:middle line:90%
the masculine pronoun for me.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:03.460 align:middle line:90%
And that's not correct."

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:05.910 align:middle line:84%
There actually are
dialogues-- if you've ever

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:12.300 align:middle line:84%
been around a small child, you
know when parents do attempt

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:18.420 align:middle line:84%
to instruct their children in
how to speak, it often-- it's

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:21.420 align:middle line:84%
not clear that the children
are paying any attention.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.600 align:middle line:84%
So here-- again, I swear
I am not making this up.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:25.830 align:middle line:90%
Somebody recorded this.

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:28.290 align:middle line:84%
A child said, "Nobody
don't like me."

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:32.130 align:middle line:84%
And the father, who should
really have had his fatherhood

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:38.190 align:middle line:84%
license taken away, said,
"No, say 'Nobody likes me.'"

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:41.070 align:middle line:84%
This dialogue repeats
six times on the tape.

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.350 align:middle line:84%
And then finally,
the child says,

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.080 align:middle line:90%
"Oh, nobody don't likes me."

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:47.730 align:middle line:90%
And curtain.

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:51.000 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what
happened after that.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.220 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this particular
dialogue is hopefully

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:56.440 align:middle line:84%
not one you'll often
hear, but you do.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.940 align:middle line:84%
There's another classic
that somebody recorded.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:04.020 align:middle line:84%
A child said, "Give
me other one spoon.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:05.760 align:middle line:90%
I want other one spoon," right?

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.760 align:middle line:84%
And the parent tries
many times to get

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.760 align:middle line:90%
them to say "the other spoon."

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:12.938 align:middle line:84%
"No, say 'the other
spoon.'" "Other one spoon."

00:15:12.938 --> 00:15:14.730 align:middle line:84%
"Say 'the other spoon.'"
"Other one spoon."

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:16.515 align:middle line:84%
"Say 'the other'"--
"No, other one spoon."

00:15:16.515 --> 00:15:17.890 align:middle line:84%
They go back and
forth like this.

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:21.080 align:middle line:84%
And the parent says one
word at a time-- "the, the,

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:23.880 align:middle line:90%
other, other, spoon, spoon."

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:25.860 align:middle line:84%
"Yeah, the other
spoon," says the child.

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:29.150 align:middle line:84%
"Now give me other one
spoon?" asks the child.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:30.950 align:middle line:90%
So parents do--

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:32.120 align:middle line:90%
I have been a parent.

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:33.830 align:middle line:84%
If you've ever been
around children-- so

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.140 align:middle line:84%
I can attest that
this is how it works.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:40.080 align:middle line:84%
Even if you are trying to
tell children how to speak,

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:42.320 align:middle line:90%
they're not listening to you.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:44.720 align:middle line:90%
And most of the time--

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:46.580 align:middle line:84%
the first dialogue
illustrates this--

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:48.753 align:middle line:84%
the parents quite
rightly are not really

00:15:48.753 --> 00:15:50.420 align:middle line:84%
paying too much
attention to the grammar

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:51.712 align:middle line:90%
of their children's utterances.

00:15:51.712 --> 00:15:53.930 align:middle line:84%
They're just excited by the
fact that their children

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:55.105 align:middle line:90%
are talking.

00:15:55.105 --> 00:15:58.620 align:middle line:90%


00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:02.760 align:middle line:84%
So how are we learning all
this complicated stuff?

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:04.920 align:middle line:84%
It's not because your
parents teach it to you.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.620 align:middle line:84%
Even when they try, you don't
pay attention, apparently,

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:09.990 align:middle line:90%
at the relevant stage.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:13.615 align:middle line:84%
And there's an answer that I've
been pushing in this class.

00:16:13.615 --> 00:16:15.600 align:middle line:84%
It's this idea of
universal grammar, which

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.540 align:middle line:84%
is that human beings
can't help building

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:20.290 align:middle line:90%
our languages in certain ways.

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:24.268 align:middle line:84%
And so you, of
course, need some data

00:16:24.268 --> 00:16:26.310 align:middle line:84%
in order to build your
language because there are

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.600 align:middle line:90%
6,000 some languages out there.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.690 align:middle line:84%
So you need to learn
which of those languages

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:32.640 align:middle line:90%
it is that you're learning.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:37.440 align:middle line:84%
But languages come with
certain properties sort

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:39.900 align:middle line:90%
of factory preset.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:42.600 align:middle line:84%
You don't actually have
to learn all this stuff

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:45.900 align:middle line:84%
about controlled PRO, and
condition B, and so on.

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:48.430 align:middle line:84%
Universal languages
just work that way.

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:50.190 align:middle line:84%
It's just a matter
of learning which

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:52.320 align:middle line:84%
of the words in your
language means "ask,"

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.570 align:middle line:84%
and which one means "see,"
and which one means "him,"

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:55.123 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:16:55.123 --> 00:16:56.790 align:middle line:84%
And once you know
those things, then you

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:58.770 align:middle line:84%
know that your language
will work this way

00:16:58.770 --> 00:16:59.940 align:middle line:90%
because you can't help it.

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:03.520 align:middle line:90%
That's the way languages work.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:06.960 align:middle line:84%
So we've spent
the whole semester

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.460 align:middle line:84%
trying to figure out exactly
what it is that you know when

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:10.710 align:middle line:90%
you know your native language.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:14.013 align:middle line:84%
And I hope I've convinced you
that it's more complicated

00:17:14.013 --> 00:17:15.180 align:middle line:90%
than you might have thought.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:18.000 align:middle line:84%
You know quite a lot,
lots of complicated stuff

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:19.170 align:middle line:90%
going on in your head.

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:22.210 align:middle line:90%


00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:25.270 align:middle line:90%
And so a question arises.

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:26.859 align:middle line:90%
What's innate?

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:28.960 align:middle line:84%
What is this stuff
that's preset?

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:30.280 align:middle line:90%
And what is learned?

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:32.020 align:middle line:84%
And as I've just
said, it's obvious

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:34.390 align:middle line:84%
that not everything is
innate because there's

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:36.280 align:middle line:84%
more than one
language out there.

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:38.680 align:middle line:84%
You've got to learn
which string of sounds

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:40.750 align:middle line:84%
means "ask," which
string of sounds

00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:43.340 align:middle line:84%
means "cat," which string
of sounds means "spoon,"

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:45.740 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:50.320 align:middle line:84%
There's some evidence also
that evidence matters,

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:52.660 align:middle line:84%
which comes from
the idea of what's

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:54.610 align:middle line:90%
called a critical period.

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:56.950 align:middle line:90%
It looks as though--

00:17:56.950 --> 00:17:59.590 align:middle line:84%
it's very hard to get
direct evidence about this,

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:01.570 align:middle line:84%
but it looks as
though there may be

00:18:01.570 --> 00:18:04.000 align:middle line:84%
a period in the
life of a child when

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:08.710 align:middle line:84%
they're especially well-designed
to acquire language.

00:18:08.710 --> 00:18:13.960 align:middle line:84%
And after that period is
over, they get worse at it.

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:16.120 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of anecdotal
evidence for this.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:20.907 align:middle line:84%
All of us fluently learn one
or more languages as a child.

00:18:20.907 --> 00:18:22.990 align:middle line:84%
When you're a small child,
you learn the languages

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.580 align:middle line:90%
that are around you.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:25.820 align:middle line:90%
For me, it was one language.

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:28.195 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of people who
learn more than one language

00:18:28.195 --> 00:18:30.190 align:middle line:90%
growing up.

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:34.390 align:middle line:84%
But anecdotally, for most
of us, once you're an adult,

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:37.570 align:middle line:84%
it's much harder to fluently
pick up another language

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:39.550 align:middle line:90%
just by being exposed to it.

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:42.520 align:middle line:84%
Even if you imagine
being surrounded

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:44.680 align:middle line:84%
by people who are willing
to just talk to you all

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:47.200 align:middle line:84%
the time in another
language, it's not clear

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:50.620 align:middle line:84%
that you would learn it in a
matter of six years or seven

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:53.770 align:middle line:84%
years, which is what you did
with your first language.

00:18:53.770 --> 00:18:58.760 align:middle line:84%
There are some cases that bear
directly on this hypothesis.

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:01.420 align:middle line:84%
They're sort of awful
cases, but I feel the need

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:03.080 align:middle line:90%
to tell you about them.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:05.920 align:middle line:84%
One of them is the
story of a child who

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.110 align:middle line:90%
was given the pseudonym Genie.

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:14.830 align:middle line:84%
Genie was discovered,
I think, in LA in 1970.

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:20.620 align:middle line:84%
She was the child of a
mother who was almost blind.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:24.260 align:middle line:84%
Her mother had a degenerative
disease of her eyes.

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:25.918 align:middle line:90%
She was gradually going blind.

00:19:25.918 --> 00:19:27.460 align:middle line:84%
And by the time
Genie was discovered,

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:30.070 align:middle line:90%
she was almost completely blind.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:35.050 align:middle line:84%
And her father was
crazy, so probably

00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:36.670 align:middle line:90%
paranoid schizophrenic.

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:41.080 align:middle line:90%
He pathologically hated noise.

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:42.320 align:middle line:90%
He couldn't stand noise.

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:44.920 align:middle line:84%
And so if you've ever been
around small children,

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:47.320 align:middle line:84%
you know that they
sometimes make noise.

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:48.102 align:middle line:90%
So he was--

00:19:48.102 --> 00:19:50.560 align:middle line:84%
I mean, talk about people who
should have had their license

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:52.480 align:middle line:90%
to be a father taken away.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:58.420 align:middle line:84%
Genie had one older sibling who
died of exposure because they

00:19:58.420 --> 00:20:04.750 align:middle line:84%
were kept in the garage of the
house because they were noisy

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:07.810 align:middle line:90%
and the father couldn't take it.

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:10.270 align:middle line:84%
She had another older
brother who did survive,

00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:11.140 align:middle line:90%
I don't know how.

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:13.960 align:middle line:84%
Maybe her father wasn't
quite as crazy when

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:15.880 align:middle line:90%
her older brother was born.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:17.992 align:middle line:84%
But when Genie
was born, they had

00:20:17.992 --> 00:20:20.200 align:middle line:84%
learned not to keep the
babies in the garage anymore.

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:24.340 align:middle line:84%
What they did was to keep her in
a dark room strapped to a chair

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:26.410 align:middle line:84%
and to punish her
for making sounds.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.660 align:middle line:90%
So no one ever spoke to her.

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:30.820 align:middle line:84%
She apparently could
sometimes hear piano music

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:32.180 align:middle line:90%
from a nearby house.

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:37.040 align:middle line:84%
So when she grew up, she
always loved piano music.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:39.040 align:middle line:84%
Her father and her older
brother would sometimes

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:40.510 align:middle line:90%
bark at her like dogs.

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:43.300 align:middle line:84%
So she was always
afraid of dogs.

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:46.600 align:middle line:84%
That went on for
the first 13 years

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:50.170 align:middle line:84%
of her life, after which she
was discovered and taken away

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:53.630 align:middle line:90%
from her parents.

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:57.070 align:middle line:84%
She was discovered because
her mother somehow--

00:20:57.070 --> 00:20:59.330 align:middle line:84%
who as I said,
was almost blind--

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:01.660 align:middle line:84%
had gotten out of
the house with Genie

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.350 align:middle line:84%
and was trying to find a
government office where

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:06.850 align:middle line:84%
somebody could help her-- not
with Genie, but with something

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:08.267 align:middle line:84%
having to do with
their blindness.

00:21:08.267 --> 00:21:11.800 align:middle line:84%
She was looking for an office
that would help her with that

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:13.690 align:middle line:84%
and wandered by accident
into an office that

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:14.830 align:middle line:90%
was about child welfare.

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.630 align:middle line:84%
And they immediately
spotted that there

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:19.480 align:middle line:90%
was something off about Genie.

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:23.620 align:middle line:84%
So Genie was taken
away from her parents--

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:29.260 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- and put in a
series of institutions,

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:32.590 align:middle line:84%
where lots of people
did their best

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:36.490 align:middle line:84%
to help her recover from the
first 13 years of her life.

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:39.010 align:middle line:84%
And she did, in a sense,
recover, in the sense

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:46.870 align:middle line:84%
that she acquired the ability
to stop attacking people when

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:47.440 align:middle line:90%
she saw them.

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:52.840 align:middle line:84%
And she was wild when she
was first out of the house.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:54.970 align:middle line:84%
And she learned
to say some words.

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:57.610 align:middle line:84%
But she never learned
to speak, in the sense

00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:59.740 align:middle line:84%
of putting words
together in anything

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:00.760 align:middle line:90%
that resembled grammar.

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:04.040 align:middle line:90%
So she was just saying words.

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:06.490 align:middle line:84%
So she knew words for
things that she wanted.

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:09.880 align:middle line:84%
And insofar as it was possible
to do intelligence tests

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:12.790 align:middle line:84%
on somebody who was as
profoundly traumatized as she

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:16.760 align:middle line:84%
was, she scored as a
perfectly intelligent person.

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:19.898 align:middle line:84%
She had a normal mind
in all kinds of ways.

00:22:19.898 --> 00:22:21.190 align:middle line:90%
But she never learned to speak.

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:23.950 align:middle line:90%


00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:26.350 align:middle line:84%
So that's one case
that people say, ah,

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:29.878 align:middle line:84%
so if you deprive a child
of linguistic information

00:22:29.878 --> 00:22:31.420 align:middle line:84%
for the beginning
of their life, then

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:33.580 align:middle line:84%
this has profound
consequences for their ability

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:35.770 align:middle line:90%
to learn to speak.

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:39.340 align:middle line:84%
And it could be that
that's what this shows.

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:43.690 align:middle line:84%
It could also be
that if you terribly

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:46.690 align:middle line:84%
traumatize someone for the
first 13 years of your life,

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:48.970 align:middle line:84%
that you damage their
ability to learn to speak.

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:52.540 align:middle line:84%
As an example of
the critical period,

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:56.800 align:middle line:84%
it lacks a certain scientific
rigor, it seems to me.

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:58.550 align:middle line:84%
So I apologize for
telling you this story.

00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:00.700 align:middle line:84%
I feel as though anyone
who takes intro linguistics

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:04.125 align:middle line:90%
should learn about Genie.

00:23:04.125 --> 00:23:05.750 align:middle line:84%
Though, as I say,
Genie is a pseudonym.

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:09.390 align:middle line:90%
I have no idea what her name is.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:10.980 align:middle line:84%
There are better
stories than that.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:14.190 align:middle line:90%
Chelsea, also a pseudonym--

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:17.790 align:middle line:84%
and actually, Chelsea's case is
a case that used to be common--

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:20.620 align:middle line:84%
not common-- used to be
more common than it is now.

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:22.158 align:middle line:84%
There are safeguards
in place now

00:23:22.158 --> 00:23:23.700 align:middle line:84%
to stop this from
happening as often.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:28.020 align:middle line:84%
Chelsea is another girl who
grew up in Northern California--

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:31.950 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what it is
about Northern California--

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:38.520 align:middle line:84%
whose parents believed that she
was profoundly developmentally

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.050 align:middle line:90%
disabled.

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:43.050 align:middle line:84%
This is not a story
like the Genie story.

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:45.570 align:middle line:84%
So her parents were perfectly
loving, caring people.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:48.420 align:middle line:84%
They were doing their
best to raise her.

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:51.150 align:middle line:90%
They treated her very well.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:53.700 align:middle line:90%
She had a happy life.

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:57.900 align:middle line:84%
But she wasn't-- she was
developmentally behind on all

00:23:57.900 --> 00:23:58.830 align:middle line:90%
kinds of milestones.

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:00.880 align:middle line:84%
In particular, she
wasn't learning to speak.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:06.810 align:middle line:84%
And so her parents just did
their best to deal with that.

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:07.860 align:middle line:90%
At some point--

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:10.440 align:middle line:84%
I want to say when
she was 12 or 13--

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:12.840 align:middle line:84%
it was discovered that
she wasn't developmentally

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:13.590 align:middle line:90%
disabled at all.

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:17.490 align:middle line:84%
She was deaf, that she
was profoundly deaf.

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:20.340 align:middle line:90%
And her parents--

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.580 align:middle line:84%
I mean, again,
perfectly kind, loving,

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:26.628 align:middle line:84%
sort of unobservant people had
never noticed this, I guess.

00:24:26.628 --> 00:24:28.170 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that
she wasn't speaking,

00:24:28.170 --> 00:24:31.050 align:middle line:84%
it wasn't because she was
developmentally disabled.

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:34.590 align:middle line:84%
It was because she
hadn't heard any speech.

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:36.393 align:middle line:84%
And nobody had
been signing to her

00:24:36.393 --> 00:24:38.310 align:middle line:84%
because they didn't
realize that she was deaf.

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:41.410 align:middle line:84%
So she hadn't been exposed to
language in any form either.

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:44.880 align:middle line:84%
And as I say, it's a happier
story than the Genie story

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.988 align:middle line:84%
because there was no
actual abuse involved.

00:24:47.988 --> 00:24:49.530 align:middle line:84%
And as I also said,
this is something

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:51.238 align:middle line:84%
that happens much less
common these days.

00:24:51.238 --> 00:24:53.820 align:middle line:84%
So all of you
hopefully were screened

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:55.140 align:middle line:90%
for deafness in schools.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.270 align:middle line:84%
And this is one of the
reasons that this happens,

00:24:57.270 --> 00:24:59.550 align:middle line:84%
is to stop this kind of
thing from happening,

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:03.960 align:middle line:84%
stop people from slipping
through the cracks in this way.

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:07.890 align:middle line:84%
Chelsea, like Genie,
wasn't exposed

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.400 align:middle line:84%
to language for the first
12 years or so of her life.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:14.370 align:middle line:84%
And Chelsea, like Genie,
never really learned to speak.

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So once they gave her
cochlear implants and she

00:25:17.310 --> 00:25:19.440 align:middle line:90%
was able to hear people--

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:21.600 align:middle line:90%
again, she learned some words.

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:25.720 align:middle line:84%
But she didn't learn how to put
words together into sentences.

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:28.580 align:middle line:84%
So she learned words like
"banana," and "cookie,"

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:29.080 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:30.360 align:middle line:84%
She knew how to say
those words when

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:31.590 align:middle line:84%
those were the things
that she wanted

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.173 align:middle line:84%
or she wanted to
comment on the world.

00:25:33.173 --> 00:25:36.780 align:middle line:84%
But she never got
past that stage.

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:38.800 align:middle line:90%
So those are two cases--

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:42.678 align:middle line:84%
one of them maybe more
convincing than the other--

00:25:42.678 --> 00:25:44.970 align:middle line:84%
that lead people to think
that there might be something

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:45.930 align:middle line:90%
like a critical period.

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:48.180 align:middle line:84%
It might really be
true that you're

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.690 align:middle line:84%
especially good at learning
language in your first seven

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:55.620 align:middle line:90%
or eight years of your life.

00:25:55.620 --> 00:26:00.600 align:middle line:84%
There are cases in
the opposite kind.

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:02.260 align:middle line:84%
If you're in the
linguistics business,

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.440 align:middle line:84%
you always hear about
these hyperpolyglots.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:06.630 align:middle line:84%
They're people who
somehow never seem

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:08.190 align:middle line:90%
to exit their critical period.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.590 align:middle line:84%
I had a professor
like this, Ken Hale,

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:13.620 align:middle line:84%
who was here when I was
a grad student here,

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:16.710 align:middle line:84%
somebody who seemed to just
be able to learn languages

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:20.340 align:middle line:84%
by being in the same room with
the speaker for a little while.

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:23.280 align:middle line:84%
He had this amazing ability
to pick up languages.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:26.800 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of
Ken Hale stories,

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:28.305 align:middle line:90%
including a story about him.

00:26:28.305 --> 00:26:31.890 align:middle line:90%


00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:34.440 align:middle line:84%
So he spent a big chunk
of the '60s wandering

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:37.440 align:middle line:84%
around Australia, going
from Aboriginal community

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:39.690 align:middle line:84%
to Aboriginal community,
learning the local languages,

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:41.860 align:middle line:84%
and studying them, and
writing grammars, and so on.

00:26:41.860 --> 00:26:44.490 align:middle line:84%
There's at least one
community that no one has

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:46.890 align:middle line:84%
been able to work
with since because he

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:48.490 align:middle line:84%
was the first person
who went there,

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:50.713 align:middle line:84%
and so he went and he was
there for like a week,

00:26:50.713 --> 00:26:52.630 align:middle line:84%
and he learned to speak
the language fluently,

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:53.338 align:middle line:90%
and then he left.

00:26:53.338 --> 00:26:55.477 align:middle line:84%
And then the next
group of linguists

00:26:55.477 --> 00:26:57.060 align:middle line:84%
came to try to work
with these people.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.980 align:middle line:84%
And the people were like,
"You people are all so slow.

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:01.897 align:middle line:84%
Why does it take you
so long to learn?

00:27:01.897 --> 00:27:04.230 align:middle line:84%
We've been telling you over
and over again these words."

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:05.970 align:middle line:84%
And linguists were like,
"Well, it takes a while

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:06.580 align:middle line:90%
to learn the language."

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:08.330 align:middle line:84%
And they're like "No,
no, no, the last guy

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:09.490 align:middle line:90%
who came through here--

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:11.250 align:middle line:90%
it was very easy.

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:13.440 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what
your problem is."

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:14.730 align:middle line:90%
Did you have a question?

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:16.272 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, is
there any evidence

00:27:16.272 --> 00:27:18.765 align:middle line:84%
of actual physical
cerebral damage

00:27:18.765 --> 00:27:21.880 align:middle line:90%
or atrophy in these cases?

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:23.980 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
for Genie, I don't

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:26.920 align:middle line:84%
know whether the relevant
scans have been done.

00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:27.980 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:30.443 align:middle line:84%
For people like Chelsea,
I also don't know.

00:27:30.443 --> 00:27:32.110 align:middle line:84%
But it would be
interesting to find out.

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:34.402 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:27:34.402 --> 00:27:35.860 align:middle line:84%
Genie-- I mean,
there are all kinds

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:39.008 align:middle line:84%
of sad things about
the story of Genie.

00:27:39.008 --> 00:27:40.550 align:middle line:84%
After she was taken
from her parents,

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:42.133 align:middle line:84%
she lived in a series
of institutions.

00:27:42.133 --> 00:27:44.770 align:middle line:90%
And then her mother--

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:46.818 align:middle line:84%
her father committed
suicide pretty soon

00:27:46.818 --> 00:27:47.860 align:middle line:90%
after she was taken away.

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:52.840 align:middle line:84%
And her mother--
eventually, I think

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:56.110 align:middle line:84%
her mother got custody
of her again for a while.

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:58.360 align:middle line:84%
And then she was back in the
institutions for a while.

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:00.610 align:middle line:84%
So she lived for a while
with some linguists

00:28:00.610 --> 00:28:05.980 align:middle line:84%
who wrote books about her,
studied her, tried to help her.

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:10.510 align:middle line:84%
And then her mother
successfully convinced the court

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:13.650 align:middle line:84%
that she would be better
off living with her mother.

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.555 align:middle line:90%
So they did that for a while.

00:28:15.555 --> 00:28:16.930 align:middle line:84%
So there was a
period there where

00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:21.040 align:middle line:84%
she was hidden from
the eyes of linguists

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:22.753 align:middle line:84%
after this initial
period, where she

00:28:22.753 --> 00:28:23.920 align:middle line:90%
was being intensely studied.

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:28.260 align:middle line:90%


00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:32.160 align:middle line:84%
OK, all right, so
it's possible anyway

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:34.632 align:middle line:84%
that there's a
stage that children

00:28:34.632 --> 00:28:36.840 align:middle line:84%
go through where they're
especially good at acquiring

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:39.540 align:middle line:84%
language, a stage that most
people then exit around six

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:41.370 align:middle line:84%
or seven years of
age, though there

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:45.150 align:middle line:84%
might be people like my old
Professor Ken Hale for whom

00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:46.950 align:middle line:90%
it just never ends.

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:50.370 align:middle line:84%
And then lots of questions
about how this knowledge works--

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:52.140 align:middle line:90%
how do you acquire it?

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:53.640 align:middle line:84%
And we've been
spending the semester

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:55.590 align:middle line:84%
talking about the nature
of this knowledge.

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:57.090 align:middle line:84%
There are people who
specialize in what's

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:58.257 align:middle line:90%
called language acquisition.

00:28:58.257 --> 00:29:02.260 align:middle line:84%
This is the study of what kids
know and when they know it.

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:04.350 align:middle line:84%
And so I wanted to tell
you a few of the results

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:05.820 align:middle line:90%
from that subdomain.

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:09.180 align:middle line:84%
There is an undergraduate
class that specializes

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:10.320 align:middle line:90%
in language acquisition.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.580 align:middle line:90%
I forget what its number is.

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:16.200 align:middle line:84%
But if this is a series of
topics that interests you,

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:19.560 align:middle line:84%
we have people who can
teach you about this.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:22.050 align:middle line:84%
There is a frequent
discovery that the people who

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:23.790 align:middle line:84%
do language
acquisition make, which

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.440 align:middle line:84%
is that children are smarter
than you might think.

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:30.180 align:middle line:84%
So they know stuff
that it's not obvious

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:34.560 align:middle line:84%
that they know just
by watching them.

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:38.678 align:middle line:84%
Lots of cases where children are
making what we may as well call

00:29:38.678 --> 00:29:40.470 align:middle line:84%
an error-- that is,
they're doing something

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:42.040 align:middle line:90%
that adults don't do.

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:45.360 align:middle line:84%
But if you look harder, you
can show that they actually

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:48.090 align:middle line:84%
do have a representation,
some knowledge that

00:29:48.090 --> 00:29:50.010 align:middle line:90%
resembles what adults do.

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:52.830 align:middle line:84%
I'll show you a
phonological example first.

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:57.870 align:middle line:84%
Here are some data
from a Japanese child.

00:29:57.870 --> 00:29:59.460 align:middle line:84%
This is a standard
representation

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:00.420 align:middle line:90%
of children's ages.

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:02.453 align:middle line:84%
So this Japanese
child, at the time

00:30:02.453 --> 00:30:03.870 align:middle line:84%
that these
observations were made,

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:06.130 align:middle line:84%
was three years
and two months old.

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:09.700 align:middle line:84%
That's what that
3:2 thing means.

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:11.640 align:middle line:84%
So observation about
this Japanese child--

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:14.970 align:middle line:84%
this Japanese child
didn't have the velar

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:16.830 align:middle line:90%
stop that Japanese has.

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:22.740 align:middle line:84%
So they were changing the
velar stops to alveolar stops--

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:23.550 align:middle line:90%
to "t"s, right?

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:25.630 align:middle line:90%
So mikaN, which is an orange--

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:28.650 align:middle line:84%
it's not exactly an orange, it's
another kind of citrus fruit--

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:30.480 align:middle line:90%
is "mitaN" for this child.

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:32.610 align:middle line:84%
Or "poketto," which is
a borrowing from English

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:34.320 align:middle line:90%
"pocket," is "potetto."

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:36.770 align:middle line:84%
Or "neko," which is
"cat," becomes "neto."

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:41.950 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so this child is
changing "k" to "t."

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:43.180 align:middle line:90%
They also have "t."

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:46.690 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese words
that have "t" in them,

00:30:46.690 --> 00:30:48.460 align:middle line:84%
like "tama," which is
"ball," or "terebi,"

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:51.910 align:middle line:84%
which is "television"--
it's another borrowing--

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:52.990 align:middle line:90%
it's a clipped borrowing.

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:55.060 align:middle line:90%
They do that a lot.

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:56.680 align:middle line:90%
They have "t" in those, too.

00:30:56.680 --> 00:30:58.720 align:middle line:84%
So this is a child who
has changed "k" to "t"

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:01.060 align:middle line:90%
and also has "t."

00:31:01.060 --> 00:31:03.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, here's the thing
about "t" in Japanese.

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:06.790 align:middle line:84%
"t" in Japanese undergoes
some phonological changes,

00:31:06.790 --> 00:31:08.260 align:middle line:84%
depending on the
following vowel.

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:13.270 align:middle line:84%
So if "t" is followed, for
example, by the vowel "a,"

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:14.320 align:middle line:90%
it's just a "t."

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:17.800 align:middle line:90%
So "doesn't wait" is "matanai."

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:19.720 align:middle line:84%
But if you put that verb
in the positive form,

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:21.460 align:middle line:84%
then the vowel that
follows the "t"

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:23.440 align:middle line:84%
won't be an "a,"
it'll be an "u"--

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:25.810 align:middle line:90%
the high back unrounded vowel.

00:31:25.810 --> 00:31:28.870 align:middle line:84%
And before "u," "t"
changes to "tsu."

00:31:28.870 --> 00:31:30.830 align:middle line:90%
That's just a general rule.

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:32.470 align:middle line:84%
If you ever learn
Japanese, you'll

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:36.250 align:middle line:84%
see it's encoded in
their writing system,

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:40.720 align:middle line:84%
in the kana, that a "t"
before an "u" is a "tsu."

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:44.335 align:middle line:84%
So "waits," the positive version
of "matanai," is "matsu."

00:31:44.335 --> 00:31:47.150 align:middle line:90%
The "t" changes to a "tsu."

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:50.080 align:middle line:84%
And if "t" is before
the vowel "i,"

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:52.840 align:middle line:84%
the high front vowel, it
[INAUDIBLE] to a "ch."

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:55.690 align:middle line:84%
So the word for "city," for
example, is "matchi," yeah,

00:31:55.690 --> 00:31:59.830 align:middle line:84%
or the formal version of
"waits" is "matchimas,"

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.330 align:middle line:84%
so you add "imas" to
"mat-," that stem "mat-,"

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:03.460 align:middle line:90%
which means "wait."

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:04.930 align:middle line:84%
You get the "t"
changing to a "ch."

00:32:04.930 --> 00:32:09.143 align:middle line:90%
OK, so basic Japanese phonology.

00:32:09.143 --> 00:32:10.060 align:middle line:90%
Now, here's the thing.

00:32:10.060 --> 00:32:12.640 align:middle line:84%
This child had all of that
basic Japanese phonology.

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:14.860 align:middle line:84%
So that child was
saying, at three years

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:18.400 align:middle line:84%
and two months, "matanai,"
"matsu," and "matchi."

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:22.180 align:middle line:84%
But this child was also
saying, for autumn, "ati."

00:32:22.180 --> 00:32:25.840 align:middle line:84%
That is, the child was
changing "k" to "t."

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:28.720 align:middle line:84%
And the child
changing "k" to "t"-

00:32:28.720 --> 00:32:31.660 align:middle line:84%
so the adult word
for autumn is "aki."

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:34.270 align:middle line:84%
And the adult word
for "bear" is "kuma."

00:32:34.270 --> 00:32:38.950 align:middle line:84%
And this child knows that "t"
becomes "ch" before "i," right?

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:42.310 align:middle line:84%
But autumn, for this
child, is not "achi."

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:43.780 align:middle line:90%
It's "ati," right?

00:32:43.780 --> 00:32:48.040 align:middle line:84%
And "bear" for this child is
not "tsuma," where "t" normally

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:51.760 align:middle line:84%
becomes "tsu" before "u"
like it does in "wait."

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:54.010 align:middle line:84%
For this child, the word
for "bear" is not "tsuma."

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:56.320 align:middle line:90%
It's "tuma," yeah?

00:32:56.320 --> 00:32:58.462 align:middle line:84%
If we were doing this in
terms of rule ordering,

00:32:58.462 --> 00:32:59.920 align:middle line:84%
I guess we could
say, yeah, this is

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:04.840 align:middle line:84%
a child for whom there is
a distinction between "t"

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:05.740 align:middle line:90%
and "k."

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:07.570 align:middle line:90%
And then there's a set of rules.

00:33:07.570 --> 00:33:10.240 align:middle line:84%
There are the rules that
change "t" to "tsu" or "ch"

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:11.740 align:middle line:90%
before certain vowels, right?

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:13.900 align:middle line:84%
And then after that,
there's another rule that

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:16.330 align:middle line:90%
changes "k" to "t."

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:19.528 align:middle line:84%
Your first thought, looking at
the first set I did, "orange,"

00:33:19.528 --> 00:33:21.070 align:middle line:84%
and "pocket," and
"cat" would be, oh,

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:25.730 align:middle line:84%
how cute this child can't say
"k," and they say "t" instead.

00:33:25.730 --> 00:33:28.960 align:middle line:84%
And that's right
in a sense, right?

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:30.550 align:middle line:90%
You might have thought then--

00:33:30.550 --> 00:33:33.040 align:middle line:84%
this is a child
who-- their Japanese

00:33:33.040 --> 00:33:36.520 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have as many sounds
in it as adult Japanese.

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:38.847 align:middle line:84%
And that's also
right, in a sense.

00:33:38.847 --> 00:33:40.930 align:middle line:84%
But there's another sense
in which it's not right.

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:44.260 align:middle line:84%
The child does distinguish
"t" and "k," right?

00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:47.800 align:middle line:84%
It's just that they
pronounce them both "t."

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:51.010 align:middle line:84%
So the "t" that's
really a "t" is

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:53.560 align:middle line:84%
the one that undergoes these
sound changes before vowels.

00:33:53.560 --> 00:33:55.873 align:middle line:84%
And the "t" that comes from
"k" is just always a "t."

00:33:55.873 --> 00:33:57.790 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter what
the following vowel is.

00:33:57.790 --> 00:34:00.760 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so there's some sense
in which this child has

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:02.200 align:middle line:90%
a full adult representation.

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:05.890 align:middle line:84%
You have to imagine them having
"aki" in their head, right?

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:08.800 align:middle line:84%
And it's just that
there is some rule that

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:11.199 align:middle line:84%
goes between their
head and their lips

00:34:11.199 --> 00:34:13.517 align:middle line:84%
that changes "k" to
"t" all the time,

00:34:13.517 --> 00:34:15.100 align:middle line:84%
no matter what the
following vowel is.

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:17.170 align:middle line:84%
There's something
like that going on.

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:20.739 align:middle line:84%
There's a small instance
of a general pattern.

00:34:20.739 --> 00:34:25.389 align:middle line:84%
What you find is kids are
doing something wrong.

00:34:25.389 --> 00:34:28.340 align:middle line:84%
They're doing something
that's not what adults do.

00:34:28.340 --> 00:34:30.130 align:middle line:84%
But if you look
very closely, you

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:32.830 align:middle line:84%
can convince yourself that they
know more than they seem to.

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:38.139 align:middle line:90%


00:34:38.139 --> 00:34:41.800 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so just to remind
you of stuff that we

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:43.850 align:middle line:90%
talked about earlier on--

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:46.900 align:middle line:84%
so what I just showed
you-- and I'll show you

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:49.389 align:middle line:84%
some more examples
like this in a second--

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:52.600 align:middle line:84%
involve just making
lots of recordings

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:55.480 align:middle line:84%
of children speaking naturally,
and then studying them.

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:59.890 align:middle line:84%
And that is one kind of thing
that acquisitionists do.

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:03.840 align:middle line:84%
There are other kinds
of work in which you

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:06.350 align:middle line:90%
run experiments on children.

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:08.410 align:middle line:84%
We talked about some
of this kind of work

00:35:08.410 --> 00:35:09.860 align:middle line:90%
early on in the class.

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:13.030 align:middle line:84%
So we're talking about
selective looking tasks

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:15.250 align:middle line:90%
and sucking rate tasks.

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:19.240 align:middle line:84%
So sucking rate tasks
make use of the fact

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:22.530 align:middle line:84%
that if you give a very small
infant something to suck on--

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:23.530 align:middle line:90%
first of all, they will.

00:35:23.530 --> 00:35:25.572 align:middle line:84%
Sucking on things is one
of their favorite things

00:35:25.572 --> 00:35:26.650 align:middle line:90%
to do at that age.

00:35:26.650 --> 00:35:29.980 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that if you
give them something to suck on,

00:35:29.980 --> 00:35:32.080 align:middle line:84%
they will suck on it at
first fairly rapidly.

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:34.030 align:middle line:84%
And then they will
start to slow down.

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:38.290 align:middle line:84%
And how quickly-- they say you
can give them something to suck

00:35:38.290 --> 00:35:42.550 align:middle line:84%
on to which you have attached a
clever device that will record

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:45.460 align:middle line:84%
the rate at which they suck,
pay attention to how often

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:46.150 align:middle line:90%
they're going--

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:49.090 align:middle line:90%
they're making sucking motions.

00:35:49.090 --> 00:35:51.340 align:middle line:84%
And the observation
is that if you

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:53.485 align:middle line:84%
give a child something
interesting to look at,

00:35:53.485 --> 00:35:55.360 align:middle line:84%
if they've started to
slow down, and then you

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:56.440 align:middle line:84%
show them an
interesting picture,

00:35:56.440 --> 00:35:57.790 align:middle line:90%
they'll speed back up again.

00:35:57.790 --> 00:35:59.440 align:middle line:90%
They perk up.

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:02.720 align:middle line:84%
And language acquisition
is to make use of this.

00:36:02.720 --> 00:36:04.390 align:middle line:90%
So if you want to know--

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:06.490 align:middle line:84%
so here's the kind of
experiment people have done.

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:09.170 align:middle line:84%
They've shown that
Japanese infants--

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:11.110 align:middle line:84%
so very small
Japanese children--

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:14.740 align:middle line:84%
can distinguish an "r"
sound from an "l" sound.

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:18.540 align:middle line:84%
That is, if you play
"rah" or "lah" at them,

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:20.540 align:middle line:84%
that they can distinguish
these from each other.

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:22.665 align:middle line:84%
This is interesting because
adult Japanese speakers

00:36:22.665 --> 00:36:24.190 align:middle line:90%
can't do that.

00:36:24.190 --> 00:36:25.840 align:middle line:84%
Japanese doesn't
have a distinction

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.280 align:middle line:90%
between "r" and "l."

00:36:27.280 --> 00:36:29.020 align:middle line:90%
They only have one liquid.

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:32.830 align:middle line:84%
So it's a famous fact
about Japanese speakers.

00:36:32.830 --> 00:36:35.470 align:middle line:84%
It's hard for them to
distinguish "r" from "l."

00:36:35.470 --> 00:36:37.390 align:middle line:84%
But very small
Japanese children--

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:39.580 align:middle line:84%
so children who are
acquiring Japanese--

00:36:39.580 --> 00:36:40.838 align:middle line:90%
can distinguish "r" from "l."

00:36:40.838 --> 00:36:42.880 align:middle line:84%
They know the difference
between "rah" and "lah."

00:36:42.880 --> 00:36:46.420 align:middle line:84%
And the way you show that is
with this sucking rate task.

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:48.285 align:middle line:84%
So you give the child
something to suck on.

00:36:48.285 --> 00:36:49.660 align:middle line:84%
And when they
start to slow down,

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:53.950 align:middle line:84%
you start playing "rah,
rah, rah, rah" at them.

00:36:53.950 --> 00:36:56.410 align:middle line:84%
And they speed up at
first because, hey, here's

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:57.400 align:middle line:90%
a new thing.

00:36:57.400 --> 00:36:58.180 align:middle line:90%
It's a recording.

00:36:58.180 --> 00:36:59.600 align:middle line:84%
And then eventually,
they slow down.

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:01.017 align:middle line:84%
They're like "rah,"
yes, I get it.

00:37:01.017 --> 00:37:02.170 align:middle line:90%
Thank you very much.

00:37:02.170 --> 00:37:04.478 align:middle line:90%
And then you switch to "lah."

00:37:04.478 --> 00:37:06.770 align:middle line:84%
So you go "rah, rah, rah,"
and they start to slow down.

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:11.020 align:middle line:84%
Then you go "lah, lah, lah,"
and they perk back up again,

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:12.490 align:middle line:84%
showing that they
can distinguish

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:14.865 align:middle line:84%
the "rah" from the "lah," that
these are different things

00:37:14.865 --> 00:37:17.200 align:middle line:90%
as far as they're concerned.

00:37:17.200 --> 00:37:18.700 align:middle line:84%
They've done similar
kinds of tests

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:23.530 align:middle line:84%
with English-acquiring
children, showing

00:37:23.530 --> 00:37:25.630 align:middle line:84%
that they can
distinguish aspirated

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:27.310 align:middle line:90%
from unaspirated stops.

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:30.080 align:middle line:84%
So cast your minds back to
the beginning of the semester.

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:33.010 align:middle line:84%
We were talking about the fact
that in a language like Hindi,

00:37:33.010 --> 00:37:34.450 align:middle line:84%
for example,
there's a difference

00:37:34.450 --> 00:37:36.820 align:middle line:84%
between "pah" [ASPIRATED]
and "pah" [UNASPIRATED]..

00:37:36.820 --> 00:37:41.120 align:middle line:84%
So English has voiced
stops and voiceless stops.

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:46.000 align:middle line:84%
We have a distinction
between "pah" and "bah."

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:48.130 align:middle line:84%
So there's an aspirated
voiceless stop.

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:49.960 align:middle line:90%
And there's a voiced stop.

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:52.690 align:middle line:84%
And then Hindi has a
three-way distinction

00:37:52.690 --> 00:37:57.400 align:middle line:84%
between "pah" [ASPIRATED] and
"pah" [UNASPIRATED] and "bah."

00:37:57.400 --> 00:38:00.892 align:middle line:84%
And so "pahl" and "pahl" are
two different words in Hindi.

00:38:00.892 --> 00:38:03.100 align:middle line:84%
I think one of them means
"knife" and the other means

00:38:03.100 --> 00:38:03.640 align:middle line:90%
"flour."

00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:05.140 align:middle line:84%
And I can't remember
which is which.

00:38:05.140 --> 00:38:07.700 align:middle line:90%
Anybody here speak Hindi?

00:38:07.700 --> 00:38:09.240 align:middle line:84%
OK, those are two
different words.

00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:16.230 align:middle line:84%
So Hindi has these two distinct
sounds that are different.

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:19.280 align:middle line:84%
And an observation
that people have

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:22.370 align:middle line:84%
made with very small infants
who are acquiring English

00:38:22.370 --> 00:38:24.740 align:middle line:84%
is that if you do the
sucking rate task with them

00:38:24.740 --> 00:38:28.670 align:middle line:84%
and you play "pah, pah, pah"
at them, that they eventually

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:29.730 align:middle line:90%
start to slow down.

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:32.171 align:middle line:84%
And if you then switch
to "pah, pah, pah,"

00:38:32.171 --> 00:38:33.920 align:middle line:84%
the unaspirated
stop, they're like,

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:36.350 align:middle line:84%
oh yeah, that's a
different thing.

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:38.990 align:middle line:84%
So they recognize that
as a different sound.

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:41.330 align:middle line:84%
One of the things
that they can show--

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:43.880 align:middle line:84%
actually, because the
beauty of aspiration

00:38:43.880 --> 00:38:48.770 align:middle line:84%
is it's the kind of thing that
comes in degrees that with--

00:38:48.770 --> 00:38:53.000 align:middle line:84%
that you have the release
of the closure, and then

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:54.620 align:middle line:84%
you have the beginning
of the vowel.

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:57.140 align:middle line:84%
And you can
electronically manipulate

00:38:57.140 --> 00:39:00.710 align:middle line:84%
exactly how many milliseconds
it takes for the vowel to start.

00:39:00.710 --> 00:39:02.760 align:middle line:84%
And there is a
particular cutoff.

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:07.250 align:middle line:84%
So if you give children
an aspirated stop

00:39:07.250 --> 00:39:10.010 align:middle line:84%
and an unaspirated stop,
they can distinguish those.

00:39:10.010 --> 00:39:13.910 align:middle line:84%
If you give them two stops that
differ in aspiration by just

00:39:13.910 --> 00:39:17.690 align:middle line:84%
a little bit, then whether
they can distinguish those two

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:20.570 align:middle line:84%
stops from each other depends
on whether one of them

00:39:20.570 --> 00:39:23.210 align:middle line:84%
is on one side and the
other on the other side

00:39:23.210 --> 00:39:24.890 align:middle line:90%
of a particular dividing line.

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:27.140 align:middle line:84%
I forget what it is, like
50 milliseconds or something

00:39:27.140 --> 00:39:27.640 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:29.360 align:middle line:84%
There's some particular
amount of time

00:39:29.360 --> 00:39:31.790 align:middle line:84%
that is the cutoff for
distinguishing sounds

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:33.440 align:middle line:90%
from each other.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:37.610 align:middle line:84%
For being able to
speak adult English,

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:39.620 align:middle line:90%
this knowledge is useless.

00:39:39.620 --> 00:39:43.190 align:middle line:84%
But the theory is
that all babies--

00:39:43.190 --> 00:39:46.340 align:middle line:84%
Japanese babies are all ready
to distinguish "l" and "r."

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:48.305 align:middle line:84%
And the English
speaking babies are all

00:39:48.305 --> 00:39:51.260 align:middle line:84%
ready to distinguish aspirated
and unaspirated stops.

00:39:51.260 --> 00:39:53.480 align:middle line:84%
The babies are just
born able to distinguish

00:39:53.480 --> 00:39:55.710 align:middle line:84%
all of the kinds of sounds
that they might need.

00:39:55.710 --> 00:39:57.260 align:middle line:84%
And then as they
grow up and start

00:39:57.260 --> 00:39:59.385 align:middle line:84%
learning the actual language
that they're learning,

00:39:59.385 --> 00:40:01.070 align:middle line:90%
they forget stuff.

00:40:01.070 --> 00:40:03.260 align:middle line:84%
Anything that isn't
useful they get rid of.

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:06.410 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese babies stop
carefully distinguishing "rah"

00:40:06.410 --> 00:40:10.154 align:middle line:84%
from "lah" because
their parents don't.

00:40:10.154 --> 00:40:12.285 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the
observations people

00:40:12.285 --> 00:40:13.410 align:middle line:90%
have made about this stuff.

00:40:13.410 --> 00:40:16.750 align:middle line:90%


00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:20.832 align:middle line:84%
So I want to get on
to the next thing.

00:40:20.832 --> 00:40:22.790 align:middle line:84%
So I don't want to spend
too much time on this.

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:25.580 align:middle line:90%


00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:27.560 align:middle line:84%
So we've spent some
time talking about stuff

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:29.870 align:middle line:84%
that suggests that
babies have just

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:33.020 align:middle line:84%
a spooky amount of
knowledge in advance,

00:40:33.020 --> 00:40:34.970 align:middle line:84%
before they learn
anything at all.

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:39.170 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, in the particular
case of acquiring phonology,

00:40:39.170 --> 00:40:41.420 align:middle line:84%
some of what they're
doing is forgetting stuff

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:43.010 align:middle line:84%
that they come into
the world knowing.

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:45.170 align:middle line:84%
That seems to be the
way they're set up.

00:40:45.170 --> 00:40:51.320 align:middle line:84%
There is stuff that children
clearly have to compute.

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:54.470 align:middle line:84%
And there's some beautiful
work on how exactly

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:56.960 align:middle line:84%
children segment
the speech stream.

00:40:56.960 --> 00:41:00.260 align:middle line:84%
So when we write
things in English,

00:41:00.260 --> 00:41:03.170 align:middle line:84%
we write with spaces
in between words.

00:41:03.170 --> 00:41:04.970 align:middle line:84%
But those spaces
don't correspond

00:41:04.970 --> 00:41:07.070 align:middle line:84%
to anything in the
phonological signal, right?

00:41:07.070 --> 00:41:09.950 align:middle line:84%
If you look at a spectrogram
of somebody speaking,

00:41:09.950 --> 00:41:12.380 align:middle line:84%
it's just a long
string of sound.

00:41:12.380 --> 00:41:14.510 align:middle line:84%
You don't put spaces
in between your words,

00:41:14.510 --> 00:41:18.890 align:middle line:84%
unless you're speaking
extremely cautiously.

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:21.050 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, children
make mistakes

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:22.070 align:middle line:90%
in speech segmentation.

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:26.000 align:middle line:84%
So a classic example
is of a child who's

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:29.880 align:middle line:84%
just been told to behave and
responds, "I am being hayve."

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:33.770 align:middle line:84%
So this is a child who thinks
that "behave" is like "be"

00:41:33.770 --> 00:41:36.380 align:middle line:84%
plus some adjective,
right, "hayve"?

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:38.138 align:middle line:90%
"Be as hayve as you can," right?

00:41:38.138 --> 00:41:39.680 align:middle line:84%
The child is doing
their best, right?

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:42.290 align:middle line:84%
Or the other
classic like this is

00:41:42.290 --> 00:41:46.340 align:middle line:84%
a child who's just been told
"We're going to go to Miami"

00:41:46.340 --> 00:41:50.090 align:middle line:84%
and responds "I don't
want to go to your Ami,"

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:51.950 align:middle line:84%
which is a similar
kind of mistake,

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:54.620 align:middle line:84%
is thinking that "Miami" is two
words, a perfectly plausible

00:41:54.620 --> 00:41:56.760 align:middle line:90%
thing.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:59.960 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds of really
interesting work on how exactly

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:02.150 align:middle line:84%
children segment
the speech stream.

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:07.100 align:middle line:84%
And there's lots of evidence
that they use statistics about

00:42:07.100 --> 00:42:12.710 align:middle line:84%
phonotactics-- that is, they
make observations like if I see

00:42:12.710 --> 00:42:17.870 align:middle line:84%
this pair of sounds next to
each other, it's because--

00:42:17.870 --> 00:42:23.960 align:middle line:84%
so in English, if I
have an expression--

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:30.440 align:middle line:84%
if I say a pair of words like
"lies beneath" the water,

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:31.790 align:middle line:90%
let's say--

00:42:31.790 --> 00:42:34.850 align:middle line:84%
so here, I've got a word ending
in a "z" and another word

00:42:34.850 --> 00:42:37.040 align:middle line:90%
beginning with a "b," yeah?

00:42:37.040 --> 00:42:39.290 align:middle line:84%
So if you're an
English speaking child,

00:42:39.290 --> 00:42:42.050 align:middle line:84%
even before you know any
words, the observation

00:42:42.050 --> 00:42:44.810 align:middle line:84%
is-- it's as though they
look at pairs like that

00:42:44.810 --> 00:42:46.970 align:middle line:84%
and they go, oh,
I don't ever seem

00:42:46.970 --> 00:42:51.500 align:middle line:84%
to hear speech streams
beginning with "zb" in English.

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:55.560 align:middle line:84%
So "zb" isn't something that
an English word can begin with.

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:58.010 align:middle line:84%
And so maybe this
is a word boundary.

00:42:58.010 --> 00:43:04.220 align:middle line:84%
And there's this experiment
that Saffran et al. did in '96

00:43:04.220 --> 00:43:06.680 align:middle line:84%
showing that very
young children are

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:10.610 align:middle line:84%
very good at-- this was,
again, using the sucking task,

00:43:10.610 --> 00:43:11.150 align:middle line:90%
I think.

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:13.460 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it was the selective
looking task, a similar kind

00:43:13.460 --> 00:43:16.910 align:middle line:84%
of task, showing
the children are

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:23.030 align:middle line:84%
very good at making observations
about which pairs of sounds

00:43:23.030 --> 00:43:25.460 align:middle line:84%
occur next to each other
frequently in a speech stream.

00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:27.710 align:middle line:84%
So in their particular
case, what they were doing

00:43:27.710 --> 00:43:31.880 align:middle line:84%
was exposing small children
to streams of syllables.

00:43:31.880 --> 00:43:34.070 align:middle line:90%
So they made up words.

00:43:34.070 --> 00:43:35.452 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to make up words, too.

00:43:35.452 --> 00:43:37.160 align:middle line:84%
So this is not the
actual words they had,

00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:38.118 align:middle line:90%
but they had the shape.

00:43:38.118 --> 00:43:40.710 align:middle line:90%
They were CV, CV, CV words.

00:43:40.710 --> 00:43:49.970 align:middle line:84%
So it was like "to, la, vi,"
and then "mu, ga, pi," whatever.

00:43:49.970 --> 00:43:53.270 align:middle line:84%
And they made up
strings of words.

00:43:53.270 --> 00:43:56.350 align:middle line:90%
Here, I'll make up one more--

00:43:56.350 --> 00:44:00.080 align:middle line:90%
"ri, na, vi."

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:02.270 align:middle line:90%
And the observation was--

00:44:02.270 --> 00:44:07.280 align:middle line:84%
so they made up speech
streams for the children

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:08.570 align:middle line:90%
that involved these words.

00:44:08.570 --> 00:44:12.440 align:middle line:84%
So the speech streams would
sound like "to la bi, mu ga bi,

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:15.130 align:middle line:84%
re na vi, re na vi, mu ga pi,
re na vi, re a vi, mu ga pi,

00:44:15.130 --> 00:44:18.650 align:middle line:90%
to la bi," without intonation.

00:44:18.650 --> 00:44:21.140 align:middle line:84%
Just these syllables
one after another.

00:44:21.140 --> 00:44:23.840 align:middle line:84%
And the children
basically very quickly got

00:44:23.840 --> 00:44:26.390 align:middle line:84%
to where they knew that
if you hear a "to,"

00:44:26.390 --> 00:44:28.640 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
hear a "la" next.

00:44:28.640 --> 00:44:31.070 align:middle line:84%
Or if you hear a "ga," you're
going to hear a "pi" next.

00:44:31.070 --> 00:44:34.940 align:middle line:84%
So they got to where they
could make those predictions.

00:44:34.940 --> 00:44:38.540 align:middle line:84%
And what that meant was
that they knew that if you

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:40.130 align:middle line:84%
heard a "pi," you
didn't know what

00:44:40.130 --> 00:44:41.297 align:middle line:90%
you were going to hear next.

00:44:41.297 --> 00:44:43.910 align:middle line:84%
There were a variety of things
that you could hear next.

00:44:43.910 --> 00:44:46.760 align:middle line:84%
So they learned these
transitional probabilities

00:44:46.760 --> 00:44:47.405 align:middle line:90%
very quickly.

00:44:47.405 --> 00:44:49.670 align:middle line:90%
And it was remarkable.

00:44:49.670 --> 00:44:51.500 align:middle line:84%
Again, babies are
smarter than they look.

00:44:51.500 --> 00:44:55.820 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the big results
of language acquisition

00:44:55.820 --> 00:44:56.480 align:middle line:90%
research.

00:44:56.480 --> 00:44:59.660 align:middle line:90%
So children are good at this.

00:44:59.660 --> 00:45:02.850 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of work since '96
on exactly what it is they're

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:03.350 align:middle line:90%
good at.

00:45:03.350 --> 00:45:05.330 align:middle line:84%
So for one thing,
the original work

00:45:05.330 --> 00:45:07.310 align:middle line:84%
involved words,
made up words that

00:45:07.310 --> 00:45:08.840 align:middle line:90%
were all three syllables long.

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:11.340 align:middle line:84%
And it was shown that if you'd
vary the number of syllables,

00:45:11.340 --> 00:45:12.838 align:middle line:90%
the babies get less good at it.

00:45:12.838 --> 00:45:15.380 align:middle line:84%
It was also shown that they get
better at it if you give them

00:45:15.380 --> 00:45:17.150 align:middle line:84%
prosodic cues, like
the words are all

00:45:17.150 --> 00:45:20.000 align:middle line:84%
stressed on the first syllable,
that that helps figure out

00:45:20.000 --> 00:45:23.660 align:middle line:90%
where the word boundaries are.

00:45:23.660 --> 00:45:26.120 align:middle line:84%
So however they're
doing it, the upshot

00:45:26.120 --> 00:45:27.770 align:middle line:90%
of it all is that children--

00:45:27.770 --> 00:45:29.843 align:middle line:84%
they speak their first
words around the end

00:45:29.843 --> 00:45:31.010 align:middle line:90%
of their first year of life.

00:45:31.010 --> 00:45:33.405 align:middle line:84%
And then by the time
they're about six years old,

00:45:33.405 --> 00:45:35.030 align:middle line:84%
for English speaking
kids, the estimate

00:45:35.030 --> 00:45:36.980 align:middle line:84%
is that they know
about 13,000 words.

00:45:36.980 --> 00:45:39.320 align:middle line:84%
So there's this period where
they're learning about six

00:45:39.320 --> 00:45:42.500 align:middle line:84%
or seven words every day,
which is astonishing.

00:45:42.500 --> 00:45:44.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you imagine
trying to learn

00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:47.660 align:middle line:84%
six or seven words every day,
go study some foreign language

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:49.820 align:middle line:84%
and try to learn six
or seven words a day.

00:45:49.820 --> 00:45:51.890 align:middle line:90%
That's a really fast clip.

00:45:51.890 --> 00:45:53.993 align:middle line:84%
But if you can do
it for six years,

00:45:53.993 --> 00:45:55.910 align:middle line:84%
then you will speak that
language really well,

00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:58.650 align:middle line:90%
whatever it is.

00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:01.470 align:middle line:84%
This has all been
about phonology.

00:46:01.470 --> 00:46:04.380 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of work on
the acquisition of syntax.

00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:09.120 align:middle line:84%
And again, this is sort
of like the Japanese child

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:12.570 align:middle line:84%
phonology that I was showing
you at the very beginning here.

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:13.810 align:middle line:90%
A similar kind of case--

00:46:13.810 --> 00:46:16.440 align:middle line:84%
there are lots of cases
of children doing things

00:46:16.440 --> 00:46:17.850 align:middle line:90%
that adults don't do.

00:46:17.850 --> 00:46:20.310 align:middle line:84%
And yet, in the
course of doing that,

00:46:20.310 --> 00:46:22.230 align:middle line:84%
demonstrating that
they know more than you

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:23.490 align:middle line:90%
might have expected them to.

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:26.250 align:middle line:84%
So children in many
languages go through what's

00:46:26.250 --> 00:46:29.040 align:middle line:90%
called a root infinitive stage.

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:34.500 align:middle line:84%
That is, they use verb
forms that are infinitives--

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:38.968 align:middle line:84%
the kind of verb that you would
use in an infinitival clause.

00:46:38.968 --> 00:46:41.010 align:middle line:84%
Here are some examples
from Danish and from Dutch

00:46:41.010 --> 00:46:42.180 align:middle line:90%
and from French.

00:46:42.180 --> 00:46:43.268 align:middle line:90%
This is not universal.

00:46:43.268 --> 00:46:45.810 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that don't
use optional root infinitives.

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:47.490 align:middle line:90%
But many languages do.

00:46:47.490 --> 00:46:49.380 align:middle line:90%
English, arguably, has it.

00:46:49.380 --> 00:46:51.210 align:middle line:84%
Of course, English
verb morphology

00:46:51.210 --> 00:46:54.090 align:middle line:84%
is so impoverished
that it's hard to see.

00:46:54.090 --> 00:47:02.640 align:middle line:84%
But the idea is that when
children say things like, what?

00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:06.150 align:middle line:84%
"Doll sleep," which
is the kind of thing

00:47:06.150 --> 00:47:11.190 align:middle line:84%
that a two-year-old might say,
that that's a way for the child

00:47:11.190 --> 00:47:13.020 align:middle line:90%
to say "The doll sleeps."

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:14.400 align:middle line:90%
And they're using an infinitive.

00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:16.650 align:middle line:84%
They're using an optional
infinitive, so a verb that's

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:18.830 align:middle line:84%
missing the morphology
that you would expect it

00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:21.690 align:middle line:90%
to have if they were an adult.

00:47:21.690 --> 00:47:25.140 align:middle line:84%
But the observation is that
although they are confused

00:47:25.140 --> 00:47:28.320 align:middle line:84%
about whether it's OK
to have a main clause be

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:30.180 align:middle line:84%
an infinitive,
what's interesting

00:47:30.180 --> 00:47:32.820 align:middle line:84%
is that they know all kinds
of sophisticated stuff

00:47:32.820 --> 00:47:35.200 align:middle line:84%
about what it would mean
for it to be an infinitive.

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:37.920 align:middle line:84%
So for example, when we
were talking about German,

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:40.600 align:middle line:84%
I think I told you
German's a V2 language.

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:44.490 align:middle line:84%
So in ordinary main clauses,
the verb is in second position.

00:47:44.490 --> 00:47:46.950 align:middle line:84%
In infinitives-- well,
infinitives are usually not

00:47:46.950 --> 00:47:48.480 align:middle line:90%
main clauses in German.

00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:51.535 align:middle line:84%
So the verb is typically
not in second position.

00:47:51.535 --> 00:47:52.785 align:middle line:90%
It's at the end of the clause.

00:47:52.785 --> 00:47:54.360 align:middle line:84%
It goes at the
end of the clause.

00:47:54.360 --> 00:47:57.420 align:middle line:84%
And German-speaking children
go through this root infinitive

00:47:57.420 --> 00:48:00.480 align:middle line:84%
stage, a stage when they're
around two or three years old,

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:02.190 align:middle line:84%
where they use
infinitival verbs a lot.

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:04.530 align:middle line:84%
And they put those
verbs where they should.

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:06.757 align:middle line:84%
So they are confused
about something.

00:48:06.757 --> 00:48:09.090 align:middle line:84%
They're confused about whether
it's OK for a main clause

00:48:09.090 --> 00:48:10.050 align:middle line:90%
to be infinitive.

00:48:10.050 --> 00:48:12.540 align:middle line:84%
But they're not confused
about the rules for where

00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:13.650 align:middle line:90%
the verb goes in German.

00:48:13.650 --> 00:48:16.590 align:middle line:84%
So when they're using-- when
they do use tense verbs--

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:18.570 align:middle line:84%
and they do-- they put
them in second position.

00:48:18.570 --> 00:48:20.520 align:middle line:84%
And when they use the
infinitival verbs,

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:22.320 align:middle line:90%
they put them at the end.

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:24.483 align:middle line:84%
And that kind of fact
has been replicated

00:48:24.483 --> 00:48:25.900 align:middle line:84%
in a bunch of
different languages.

00:48:25.900 --> 00:48:29.085 align:middle line:84%
So here's a German
two-year-old saying--

00:48:29.085 --> 00:48:31.095 align:middle line:84%
the child wants to say
"Thorsten has Caesar"--

00:48:31.095 --> 00:48:34.260 align:middle line:90%


00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:37.500 align:middle line:84%
I think Thorsten
was a teddy bear--

00:48:37.500 --> 00:48:39.720 align:middle line:90%
and "have" is in the infinitive.

00:48:39.720 --> 00:48:41.400 align:middle line:90%
And so it's at the end.

00:48:41.400 --> 00:48:44.640 align:middle line:84%
But in that second example,
the child is trying to say,

00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:46.027 align:middle line:90%
"I have a big ball."

00:48:46.027 --> 00:48:48.360 align:middle line:84%
And that's pretty good German,
apart from mispronouncing

00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:50.340 align:middle line:90%
the word for "big."

00:48:50.340 --> 00:48:52.920 align:middle line:84%
The word for "have"
is in second position.

00:48:52.920 --> 00:48:54.120 align:middle line:90%
It's inflected.

00:48:54.120 --> 00:48:55.200 align:middle line:90%
It's not infinitival.

00:48:55.200 --> 00:48:58.560 align:middle line:90%
And it goes in second position.

00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:01.560 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, this child is
acting like an adult German,

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:05.520 align:middle line:84%
except for the fact about having
an infinitive in a main clause.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:09.000 align:middle line:84%
So there's a mistake, but it's
a constrained kind of mistake.

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:09.990 align:middle line:90%
And this is pretty--

00:49:09.990 --> 00:49:12.540 align:middle line:90%


00:49:12.540 --> 00:49:15.060 align:middle line:84%
again, this is-- we're
back now to studying

00:49:15.060 --> 00:49:18.760 align:middle line:84%
this stuff by just studying
what children actually say.

00:49:18.760 --> 00:49:21.480 align:middle line:84%
This is a recording study
of this German child,

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:24.540 align:middle line:84%
Andreas, who was
two at the time--

00:49:24.540 --> 00:49:26.130 align:middle line:90%
281 utterances.

00:49:26.130 --> 00:49:32.520 align:middle line:84%
And a healthy proportion of them
were these root infinitives.

00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:35.010 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
them, it turns out

00:49:35.010 --> 00:49:37.470 align:middle line:84%
that the finite verbs are in
the second position mostly

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:40.770 align:middle line:84%
and the infinitives are
in final position mostly.

00:49:40.770 --> 00:49:43.080 align:middle line:84%
So he's confused about where
he can use infinitives.

00:49:43.080 --> 00:49:45.205 align:middle line:84%
But he knows what to do
with them once he has them.

00:49:45.205 --> 00:49:49.750 align:middle line:90%


00:49:49.750 --> 00:49:52.330 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, we have time
for this, I think.

00:49:52.330 --> 00:49:56.140 align:middle line:84%
OK, so all of this has
been about studying

00:49:56.140 --> 00:49:58.120 align:middle line:90%
children's naturalistic output.

00:49:58.120 --> 00:50:01.930 align:middle line:84%
That is, it's been about
studying what children actually

00:50:01.930 --> 00:50:03.940 align:middle line:84%
say, so making
careful recordings.

00:50:03.940 --> 00:50:08.140 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of this involves
putting tape recorders

00:50:08.140 --> 00:50:10.480 align:middle line:84%
in the homes of parents
of young children.

00:50:10.480 --> 00:50:13.740 align:middle line:90%
The parents volunteer for this.

00:50:13.740 --> 00:50:14.740 align:middle line:90%
Hopefully, they're paid.

00:50:14.740 --> 00:50:15.670 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
they're paid or not.

00:50:15.670 --> 00:50:18.190 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, recording devices
are put in their houses.

00:50:18.190 --> 00:50:20.140 align:middle line:84%
And you end up with
many, many recordings

00:50:20.140 --> 00:50:23.125 align:middle line:84%
of children speaking,
parents speaking to children.

00:50:23.125 --> 00:50:24.790 align:middle line:84%
And you transcribe
these recordings.

00:50:24.790 --> 00:50:27.700 align:middle line:84%
There's an online
database of a lot

00:50:27.700 --> 00:50:29.230 align:middle line:90%
of these classic recordings.

00:50:29.230 --> 00:50:34.000 align:middle line:84%
It's called the
CHILDES database, which

00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:36.100 align:middle line:90%
I think is publicly accessible.

00:50:36.100 --> 00:50:40.310 align:middle line:84%
Actually, if you Google it,
you can probably find it.

00:50:40.310 --> 00:50:42.640 align:middle line:84%
But there's another kind
of work that people do--

00:50:42.640 --> 00:50:45.940 align:middle line:84%
and we have a lab here at
MIT where this kind of work

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:47.440 align:middle line:90%
is being done--

00:50:47.440 --> 00:50:49.540 align:middle line:84%
where you do what you could
think of as consultant

00:50:49.540 --> 00:50:54.178 align:middle line:84%
work, kind of the work you're
doing with the languages

00:50:54.178 --> 00:50:55.720 align:middle line:84%
that you've been
doing field work on,

00:50:55.720 --> 00:50:59.680 align:middle line:84%
except the children
are probably more

00:50:59.680 --> 00:51:05.390 align:middle line:84%
likely to cry and to demand
cookies and things like that.

00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:08.530 align:middle line:84%
So some of this
consultant work involves

00:51:08.530 --> 00:51:11.170 align:middle line:90%
showing a child a story--

00:51:11.170 --> 00:51:13.090 align:middle line:84%
either telling them a
story or showing them

00:51:13.090 --> 00:51:15.190 align:middle line:84%
a series of pictures that
demonstrate the story--

00:51:15.190 --> 00:51:20.150 align:middle line:84%
and then asking them whether
something is true or not.

00:51:20.150 --> 00:51:24.760 align:middle line:84%
So one classic way to do
this kind of experiment-- you

00:51:24.760 --> 00:51:27.760 align:middle line:84%
have somebody who is
telling the story,

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:30.160 align:middle line:84%
and then another experimenter
who has a puppet.

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:32.920 align:middle line:84%
And the child's job is to tell
the puppet whether the puppet

00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:34.852 align:middle line:90%
is right or wrong.

00:51:34.852 --> 00:51:36.310 align:middle line:84%
The people who do
this kind of work

00:51:36.310 --> 00:51:39.280 align:middle line:84%
have discovered it's easier to
get a child to tell a puppet

00:51:39.280 --> 00:51:42.100 align:middle line:84%
that the puppet is wrong than to
tell an adult that the adult is

00:51:42.100 --> 00:51:42.970 align:middle line:90%
wrong.

00:51:42.970 --> 00:51:45.700 align:middle line:84%
So you have the puppet
say, I know what happened.

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:47.800 align:middle line:84%
And then the puppet
describes what happened.

00:51:47.800 --> 00:51:51.310 align:middle line:84%
And that way, you
learn whether the child

00:51:51.310 --> 00:51:54.400 align:middle line:84%
thinks that the sentence
that the puppet utters

00:51:54.400 --> 00:51:57.920 align:middle line:84%
is a true description of
what has happened or not.

00:51:57.920 --> 00:52:00.380 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of
work, for example,

00:52:00.380 --> 00:52:03.160 align:middle line:84%
on something that children do,
which is kind of interesting.

00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:09.100 align:middle line:84%
But if you show
children a picture--

00:52:09.100 --> 00:52:11.350 align:middle line:84%
I've done this work
with five-year-olds.

00:52:11.350 --> 00:52:14.020 align:middle line:90%
This goes on fairly late.

00:52:14.020 --> 00:52:21.910 align:middle line:84%
If you show children a picture
of, let's say, four elephants--

00:52:21.910 --> 00:52:24.590 align:middle line:84%
and now, you will
have to apologize--

00:52:24.590 --> 00:52:29.170 align:middle line:84%
you will have to forgive my
inability to draw an elephant.

00:52:29.170 --> 00:52:31.600 align:middle line:84%
Please pretend that
these are elephants.

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:34.028 align:middle line:84%
Here, let's make it a story
about three elephants.

00:52:34.028 --> 00:52:36.070 align:middle line:84%
So there are three elephants,
you tell the child.

00:52:36.070 --> 00:52:40.480 align:middle line:90%
And you tell them there are--

00:52:40.480 --> 00:52:42.490 align:middle line:90%
and there are rabbits sitting--

00:52:42.490 --> 00:52:44.230 align:middle line:90%
here some rabbits.

00:52:44.230 --> 00:52:46.720 align:middle line:84%
Why am I telling
the story this way?

00:52:46.720 --> 00:52:50.724 align:middle line:84%
Why did I not put these
pictures on the slide?

00:52:50.724 --> 00:52:53.225 align:middle line:84%
OK, and there are rabbits
on two of the elephants.

00:52:53.225 --> 00:52:53.725 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:52:53.725 --> 00:52:56.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Those are remarkably
good elephants and rabbits.

00:52:56.410 --> 00:52:57.550 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh
thank you very much.

00:52:57.550 --> 00:52:58.150 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:52:58.150 --> 00:52:59.530 align:middle line:84%
After all that
self-deprecation, that's

00:52:59.530 --> 00:53:01.030 align:middle line:84%
exactly what I was hoping
somebody would say.

00:53:01.030 --> 00:53:02.822 align:middle line:84%
Please write that on
the course evaluation:

00:53:02.822 --> 00:53:05.630 align:middle line:84%
"Surprisingly good at drawing
elephants and rabbits."

00:53:05.630 --> 00:53:07.510 align:middle line:84%
So here are three
elephants and two rabbits.

00:53:07.510 --> 00:53:09.823 align:middle line:84%
You show a child a
picture like this that has

00:53:09.823 --> 00:53:10.990 align:middle line:90%
elephants and rabbits in it.

00:53:10.990 --> 00:53:16.060 align:middle line:84%
And you ask-- the puppet says,
"I know what's happening.

00:53:16.060 --> 00:53:20.275 align:middle line:84%
Every rabbit is
riding an elephant."

00:53:20.275 --> 00:53:25.310 align:middle line:90%


00:53:25.310 --> 00:53:28.010 align:middle line:84%
And an adult-- you also do this
experiment with adults, just

00:53:28.010 --> 00:53:28.760 align:middle line:90%
to have a control.

00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:31.135 align:middle line:84%
So you tell the adults, this
is going to be for children,

00:53:31.135 --> 00:53:32.083 align:middle line:90%
but please humor us.

00:53:32.083 --> 00:53:32.750 align:middle line:90%
Here's a puppet.

00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:34.790 align:middle line:84%
You have to tell the puppet
whether it's right or wrong.

00:53:34.790 --> 00:53:36.060 align:middle line:84%
The puppet looks at
this picture and says,

00:53:36.060 --> 00:53:36.977 align:middle line:90%
"I know what happened.

00:53:36.977 --> 00:53:38.750 align:middle line:84%
Every rabbit is
riding an elephant."

00:53:38.750 --> 00:53:41.480 align:middle line:90%
And adults will say yes.

00:53:41.480 --> 00:53:44.090 align:middle line:84%
But children at a
certain age will say no.

00:53:44.090 --> 00:53:46.940 align:middle line:84%
And then when you ask
them why not, they'll

00:53:46.940 --> 00:53:49.490 align:middle line:84%
point at this elephant
and say, "This elephant

00:53:49.490 --> 00:53:51.687 align:middle line:90%
doesn't have a rabbit."

00:53:51.687 --> 00:53:53.270 align:middle line:84%
And so people are
trying to figure out

00:53:53.270 --> 00:53:55.910 align:middle line:84%
what is going on in the
heads of these children.

00:53:55.910 --> 00:53:57.995 align:middle line:90%
Why are they doing this?

00:53:57.995 --> 00:53:59.870 align:middle line:84%
So this is an active
area of research, trying

00:53:59.870 --> 00:54:00.620 align:middle line:90%
to figure out what's going on.

00:54:00.620 --> 00:54:01.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:54:01.120 --> 00:54:04.318 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Isn't that complicated
by syntax [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:04.318 --> 00:54:06.110 align:middle line:84%
because when I first
heard that [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:06.110 --> 00:54:10.243 align:middle line:90%
was a nightmare [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:10.243 --> 00:54:11.660 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Oh yeah, you were

00:54:11.660 --> 00:54:12.890 align:middle line:84%
traumatized by the
guards standing

00:54:12.890 --> 00:54:13.932 align:middle line:90%
in front of the building.

00:54:13.932 --> 00:54:15.350 align:middle line:90%
I'm so sorry.

00:54:15.350 --> 00:54:16.910 align:middle line:84%
Every rabbit is
riding an elephant.

00:54:16.910 --> 00:54:21.650 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so the thing
is the children--

00:54:21.650 --> 00:54:22.840 align:middle line:90%
how shall I say this?

00:54:22.840 --> 00:54:24.890 align:middle line:84%
I mean, so first of all,
if you have the puppet

00:54:24.890 --> 00:54:27.872 align:middle line:84%
say this, the children-- so
the children will say, no,

00:54:27.872 --> 00:54:28.580 align:middle line:90%
that's not right.

00:54:28.580 --> 00:54:31.400 align:middle line:84%
And when you ask them why not,
they point at the elephant.

00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:32.900 align:middle line:90%
They don't panic.

00:54:32.900 --> 00:54:34.780 align:middle line:90%
They don't like start to cry.

00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:35.780 align:middle line:90%
I mean, some of them do.

00:54:35.780 --> 00:54:36.810 align:middle line:90%
You exclude those.

00:54:36.810 --> 00:54:38.660 align:middle line:84%
But the ones who can
handle this experiment,

00:54:38.660 --> 00:54:44.810 align:middle line:84%
they have a theory about what
the sentence means, apparently.

00:54:44.810 --> 00:54:48.800 align:middle line:84%
And then if you give children
another picture which is just

00:54:48.800 --> 00:54:51.220 align:middle line:84%
like this one but
there's an extra rabbit,

00:54:51.220 --> 00:54:54.410 align:middle line:84%
then the adults--
there's the third rabbit.

00:54:54.410 --> 00:54:57.500 align:middle line:84%
Then the adult-- that's about
as large as an elephant.

00:54:57.500 --> 00:54:59.450 align:middle line:84%
Then the adults and
the children agree.

00:54:59.450 --> 00:55:01.160 align:middle line:84%
They all say, oh no,
now it's not true.

00:55:01.160 --> 00:55:03.410 align:middle line:84%
It's not true that every
rabbit is riding an elephant.

00:55:03.410 --> 00:55:07.220 align:middle line:84%
So children apparently have
some-- at a certain age,

00:55:07.220 --> 00:55:08.990 align:middle line:84%
have something
interesting that they're

00:55:08.990 --> 00:55:11.930 align:middle line:84%
doing with qualifiers that
is not what adults are doing.

00:55:11.930 --> 00:55:12.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:55:12.910 --> 00:55:15.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In this
picture with the rabbit,

00:55:15.320 --> 00:55:18.770 align:middle line:84%
do the children point to the
rabbit [INAUDIBLE] not true

00:55:18.770 --> 00:55:19.340 align:middle line:90%
always?

00:55:19.340 --> 00:55:20.017 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:55:20.017 --> 00:55:22.100 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Or do
they point to the elephant?

00:55:22.100 --> 00:55:22.760 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:55:22.760 --> 00:55:24.552 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I should also
have said, you can also

00:55:24.552 --> 00:55:27.290 align:middle line:84%
point-- you should give them
a picture like this, where

00:55:27.290 --> 00:55:31.970 align:middle line:84%
every elephant has-- where
there isn't an extra elephant,

00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:33.260 align:middle line:90%
but there is an extra rabbit.

00:55:33.260 --> 00:55:35.300 align:middle line:84%
And then again, they will
say, "No, that's not true."

00:55:35.300 --> 00:55:36.200 align:middle line:90%
And they'll point to the rabbit.

00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:37.550 align:middle line:84%
I actually don't
know if you give them

00:55:37.550 --> 00:55:39.650 align:middle line:84%
an extra of both of them
whether they always point

00:55:39.650 --> 00:55:40.850 align:middle line:90%
the elephant or the rabbit.

00:55:40.850 --> 00:55:44.582 align:middle line:84%
That's a good question,
interesting to find out.

00:55:44.582 --> 00:55:46.540 align:middle line:84%
So this is the kind of
thing that people study.

00:55:46.540 --> 00:55:49.050 align:middle line:84%
They do all this interesting
work with children,

00:55:49.050 --> 00:55:50.800 align:middle line:84%
try to figure out what
exactly is going on

00:55:50.800 --> 00:55:52.330 align:middle line:90%
in their little heads.

00:55:52.330 --> 00:55:56.650 align:middle line:84%
And one of the big
takeaways from this research

00:55:56.650 --> 00:55:58.150 align:middle line:84%
is that they-- first
of all, they're

00:55:58.150 --> 00:56:01.990 align:middle line:84%
not like adults, which any
parent could have told them.

00:56:01.990 --> 00:56:04.300 align:middle line:84%
But also, although they
are not like adults,

00:56:04.300 --> 00:56:08.410 align:middle line:84%
they have mistakes of certain
kinds and other kinds of things

00:56:08.410 --> 00:56:09.910 align:middle line:84%
that they don't
make mistakes about.

00:56:09.910 --> 00:56:11.860 align:middle line:84%
So they may use
optional infinitives,

00:56:11.860 --> 00:56:15.417 align:middle line:84%
if they're learning a language
that has optional infinitives.

00:56:15.417 --> 00:56:17.500 align:middle line:84%
But they put the infinitives
where the infinitives

00:56:17.500 --> 00:56:18.667 align:middle line:90%
ought to go in the sentence.

00:56:18.667 --> 00:56:23.380 align:middle line:84%
They've got a particular thing
that they're wrong about.

00:56:23.380 --> 00:56:25.570 align:middle line:84%
And it's for a particular
stage of their life.

00:56:25.570 --> 00:56:26.920 align:middle line:90%
They eventually age out of it.

00:56:26.920 --> 00:56:29.920 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly here, this is
a very common phenomenon,

00:56:29.920 --> 00:56:34.360 align:middle line:84%
that children, if you show
them this kind of picture,

00:56:34.360 --> 00:56:38.980 align:middle line:84%
will say "No, no" because
of the extra elephant.

00:56:38.980 --> 00:56:41.200 align:middle line:84%
And we're still
trying to figure out

00:56:41.200 --> 00:56:44.180 align:middle line:84%
what exactly it is that children
are confused about there.

00:56:44.180 --> 00:56:46.270 align:middle line:90%
So there's lots of work to do.

00:56:46.270 --> 00:56:48.190 align:middle line:84%
If you are interested
in linguistics

00:56:48.190 --> 00:56:49.782 align:middle line:84%
and interested in
children, as I say,

00:56:49.782 --> 00:56:51.740 align:middle line:84%
there's a lab that works
on this kind of thing.

00:56:51.740 --> 00:56:53.590 align:middle line:84%
And they're always
looking for volunteers,

00:56:53.590 --> 00:56:55.300 align:middle line:90%
people who want to help out.

00:56:55.300 --> 00:56:57.100 align:middle line:84%
And there is a
class specifically

00:56:57.100 --> 00:56:59.830 align:middle line:84%
about language acquisition,
undergrad level class

00:56:59.830 --> 00:57:02.860 align:middle line:84%
for language acquisition,
where people study

00:57:02.860 --> 00:57:05.290 align:middle line:90%
how to do this kind of thing.

00:57:05.290 --> 00:57:07.823 align:middle line:90%
Questions about any of this?

00:57:07.823 --> 00:57:09.240 align:middle line:84%
When I put up the
slides, I'll try

00:57:09.240 --> 00:57:12.950 align:middle line:84%
to find the actual pictures with
the elephants and the rabbits.

00:57:12.950 --> 00:57:16.580 align:middle line:84%
OK, all right, well then, let's
end a little bit early today.

00:57:16.580 --> 00:57:20.700 align:middle line:84%
Next week, we will
start up on Tuesday.

00:57:20.700 --> 00:57:21.680 align:middle line:90%
It'll be our last day.

00:57:21.680 --> 00:57:24.170 align:middle line:84%
I'm debating-- oh, actually,
maybe I can get feedback

00:57:24.170 --> 00:57:25.250 align:middle line:90%
from you guys about this.

00:57:25.250 --> 00:57:28.100 align:middle line:84%
On our last day, there are
two things that I could do.

00:57:28.100 --> 00:57:31.730 align:middle line:84%
I could try to do a summary of
everything that we have done

00:57:31.730 --> 00:57:35.270 align:middle line:84%
and, well, probably a brief
summary in which we wouldn't

00:57:35.270 --> 00:57:37.970 align:middle line:84%
talk for very long,
and encourage you again

00:57:37.970 --> 00:57:41.870 align:middle line:84%
to do the evaluation forms,
and then send you on your way.

00:57:41.870 --> 00:57:46.820 align:middle line:84%
Or I could do a class
on signed language,

00:57:46.820 --> 00:57:49.650 align:middle line:84%
which is a topic that I haven't
gotten to during the semester.

00:57:49.650 --> 00:57:53.040 align:middle line:84%
But we could spend a
spend a class doing that.

00:57:53.040 --> 00:57:54.870 align:middle line:84%
Think about that and
send me an email.

00:57:54.870 --> 00:57:56.750 align:middle line:84%
Some of you are using
your facial expressions

00:57:56.750 --> 00:57:59.167 align:middle line:84%
to give me ideas about which
of those things you'd prefer.

00:57:59.167 --> 00:58:01.565 align:middle line:84%
But send me email if you
have a preference one way

00:58:01.565 --> 00:58:03.690 align:middle line:84%
or another about which of
those things I should do.

00:58:03.690 --> 00:58:06.740 align:middle line:84%
And I will take your
preferences into account.

00:58:06.740 --> 00:58:10.630 align:middle line:84%
All right, have a
good rest of your day.

00:58:10.630 --> 00:58:15.000 align:middle line:90%